1
00:00:07,172 --> 00:00:11,172
Hi . I 'll be your host for this commentary .

2
00:00:11,297 --> 00:00:13,838
My name is Masato Kobayashi . I 'm a film writer .

3
00:00:13,963 --> 00:00:16,797
Let me introduce our guests :

4
00:00:16,922 --> 00:00:19,755
he director , Takashi Mike , and the screenwriter , Daisuke Tengan .

5
00:00:19,880 --> 00:00:20,922
Hi .

6
00:00:21,047 --> 00:00:25,047
Should I say " Nice to meet you " or " Good evening " ?

7
00:00:25,172 --> 00:00:28,255
I 'm guessing most people won 't be watching this during the daytime .

8
00:00:28,380 --> 00:00:30,630
- Good evening . I 'm Miike . - Hi , Tengan here .

9
00:00:32,088 --> 00:00:39,088
This year marks the 10th anniversary of Audition .

10
00:00:39,213 --> 00:00:42,922
Many of Mike 's films have been successful abroad ,

11
00:00:43,047 --> 00:00:47,713
but Audition is especially popular .

12
00:00:48,713 --> 00:00:54,880
This film has influenced horror writers all over the world .

13
00:00:56,380 --> 00:00:59,922
In 2007 , Time magazine named Audition

14
00:01:00,047 --> 00:01:02,922
one of the top 25 scariest horror movies ,

15
00:01:03,047 --> 00:01:06,172
alongside the likes of Halloween ,

16
00:01:06,297 --> 00:01:10,630
The Texas Chain Saw Massacre , and Bambi .

17
00:01:10,755 --> 00:01:14,630
How did you feel about that ?

18
00:01:14,755 --> 00:01:16,755
Well ...

19
00:01:19,088 --> 00:01:22,922
I didn 't make this film to compete at international festivals

20
00:01:23,047 --> 00:01:27,380
or to make money overseas .

21
00:01:27,505 --> 00:01:31,422
I just wanted to do the best job I could do

22
00:01:31,547 --> 00:01:34,047
while staying under budget

23
00:01:34,172 --> 00:01:37,213
and still getting some enjoyment out of the filmmaking process ,

24
00:01:37,338 --> 00:01:41,172
so it makes me very happy

25
00:01:41,297 --> 00:01:43,463
for the film to get such recognition .

26
00:01:43,588 --> 00:01:46,713
I feel like I 'm Yoji Takita with his movie Departures .

27
00:01:46,838 --> 00:01:52,338
Well , they probably won 't know what I 'm talking about .

28
00:01:52,463 --> 00:01:54,630
That 's a timely topic !

29
00:01:54,755 --> 00:01:57,463
You know , it 's been a while .

30
00:01:57,588 --> 00:02:00,672
And I 've been drinking a lot lately ,

31
00:02:00,797 --> 00:02:04,963
so you 're going to have to forgive me if I remember things incorrectly

32
00:02:05,088 --> 00:02:07,255
or if I get something wrong .

33
00:02:07,380 --> 00:02:09,755
- Looking forward to that . - Okay .

34
00:02:09,880 --> 00:02:12,130
Tengan , how do you feel about the film 's success ?

35
00:02:12,255 --> 00:02:16,963
As I recall , when I accepted the job ,

36
00:02:17,088 --> 00:02:21,338
there were a lot of Japanese horror movies being made .

37
00:02:21,463 --> 00:02:28,505
I remember Miike told me that there was no sense in making another one .

38
00:02:28,630 --> 00:02:32,505
I think that horror movies are generally done

39
00:02:32,630 --> 00:02:37,088
by filmmakers who genuinely love horror movies

40
00:02:37,213 --> 00:02:40,547
and who specialise in making horror movies .

41
00:02:40,672 --> 00:02:44,880
Neither of us specialised in horror movies ,

42
00:02:45,005 --> 00:02:50,172
so we knew right away that we 'd have a different approach .

43
00:02:50,297 --> 00:02:52,588
As a result ...

44
00:02:54,172 --> 00:02:57,297
I was chosen for Masters of Horror .

45
00:02:58,213 --> 00:03:03,755
And again , we wanted Tengan to write . We didn 't want anyone else .

46
00:03:03,880 --> 00:03:08,422
Even for this genre , we wanted Tengan .

47
00:03:08,547 --> 00:03:11,880
And I don 't specialise in horror movies .

48
00:03:12,005 --> 00:03:15,588
Oh , yeah , I 'm going to ask you about Imprint later .

49
00:03:15,713 --> 00:03:17,213
Well ...

50
00:03:18,713 --> 00:03:23,255
The original story was by Ryu Murakami .

51
00:03:23,380 --> 00:03:25,838
You adapted his book to film .

52
00:03:25,963 --> 00:03:29,422
Did you know the story before you started production ?

53
00:03:31,630 --> 00:03:35,630
I had heard of it .

54
00:03:35,755 --> 00:03:37,297
I had not read it yet .

55
00:03:38,338 --> 00:03:40,047
Yeah , me neither .

56
00:03:40,172 --> 00:03:43,422
So how and when did this all get started ?

57
00:03:43,547 --> 00:03:45,547
Well ...

58
00:03:45,672 --> 00:03:48,713
There 's this company , Omega Project . It 's a unique company .

59
00:03:48,838 --> 00:03:51,922
And there was this guy , Yokohama .

60
00:03:52,047 --> 00:03:54,672
He wasn 't in the film industry .

61
00:03:54,797 --> 00:03:58,088
He started this company

62
00:03:58,213 --> 00:04:01,088
as a way of making money .

63
00:04:01,213 --> 00:04:04,088
And this was a so-called " independent film . "

64
00:04:04,213 --> 00:04:07,547
He liked the novel .

65
00:04:07,672 --> 00:04:12,672
He thought the story was interesting , and he thought it 'd be a good movie .

66
00:04:12,797 --> 00:04:16,755
So , he teamed up with Shindo , our producer .

67
00:04:16,880 --> 00:04:21,797
They talked to Murakami , and they got the green light from him .

68
00:04:21,922 --> 00:04:25,505
They discussed who they thought would be best suited to direct .

69
00:04:25,630 --> 00:04:28,755
I think that 's how it started .

70
00:04:28,880 --> 00:04:32,338
So , we weren 't the ones who brought in the story .

71
00:04:39,672 --> 00:04:45,963
How was the original book adapted for the film ?

72
00:04:46,088 --> 00:04:49,380
I think it 's close to the original story .

73
00:04:49,505 --> 00:04:54,047
At least in the first half , it 's very close to the original story .

74
00:04:55,255 --> 00:04:59,380
You did a good job at making it feel more faithful than it is , Tengan .

75
00:04:59,505 --> 00:05:01,297
Right , right .

76
00:05:02,005 --> 00:05:08,338
In the beginning , Murakami had a lot of ideas ,

77
00:05:08,463 --> 00:05:13,172
a lot of opinions about how it should be made .

78
00:05:13,297 --> 00:05:17,380
Since it 's his story , it 's his baby , but also because he 's a director , too ,

79
00:05:17,505 --> 00:05:21,255
so I pretended to listen .

80
00:05:21,380 --> 00:05:25,755
When we finished the film , we brought it to Murakami .

81
00:05:25,880 --> 00:05:30,005
Right when it was completed , there was a screening for Murakami only .

82
00:05:30,130 --> 00:05:32,088
We weren 't even there .

83
00:05:32,213 --> 00:05:35,630
But just after the screening , he called me , and he was very excited .

84
00:05:35,755 --> 00:05:39,130
He said , " This is awesome . This is great ! "

85
00:05:39,255 --> 00:05:45,297
Apparently , he liked the scenes we changed from the original story .

86
00:05:45,422 --> 00:05:50,338
I got the idea that he was willing to trust other people

87
00:05:50,463 --> 00:05:54,755
to turn his story to a movie .

88
00:05:57,672 --> 00:06:03,297
But the basic story was well-written . We put that into the script .

89
00:06:04,755 --> 00:06:10,255
When you think about it , all we really had to do

90
00:06:10,380 --> 00:06:14,130
was add actors and sets to the equation , make it real .

91
00:06:15,338 --> 00:06:19,255
That 's how we made this film .

92
00:06:19,380 --> 00:06:23,297
Of all the movies I 've made ,

93
00:06:23,422 --> 00:06:25,672
very few of them stick so closely to the script ,

94
00:06:25,797 --> 00:06:28,338
but this was one of them .

95
00:06:30,755 --> 00:06:32,963
Some scenes toward the end did end up

96
00:06:33,088 --> 00:06:35,130
being different from the original story .

97
00:06:35,255 --> 00:06:38,922
I used some more cinematic plots .

98
00:06:40,088 --> 00:06:44,422
It is pretty different from your typical horror movie ...

99
00:06:45,380 --> 00:06:49,297
but I think that 's what made it interesting .

100
00:06:49,422 --> 00:06:55,755
So , do you not consider Audition to be " horror " ?

101
00:06:55,880 --> 00:06:59,547
Well , usually horror movies deal with ...

102
00:07:01,130 --> 00:07:04,838
something supernatural or demonic or whatever .

103
00:07:10,255 --> 00:07:12,172
So , how do I put this ?

104
00:07:14,005 --> 00:07:15,880
People get spooked by that .

105
00:07:17,213 --> 00:07:20,630
They shake in fear .

106
00:07:20,755 --> 00:07:25,380
From my point of view , humans are much scarier .

107
00:07:26,672 --> 00:07:30,297
Humans can be demons or angels .

108
00:07:30,422 --> 00:07:32,838
So , in this movie ...

109
00:07:34,172 --> 00:07:35,463
well ...

110
00:07:35,588 --> 00:07:38,505
all the characters in this are just regular folks ,

111
00:07:38,630 --> 00:07:42,047
but regular folks have dark sides ,

112
00:07:42,172 --> 00:07:46,463
and here those dark sides are portrayed in kind of a bizarre way .

113
00:07:48,005 --> 00:07:54,963
Aoyama ends up like he does , and he is responsible for that .

114
00:07:55,088 --> 00:07:58,130
One common theme among these characters

115
00:07:58,255 --> 00:08:01,422
is that they just want to be happy .

116
00:08:04,255 --> 00:08:08,630
Usually Japanese horror movies

117
00:08:08,755 --> 00:08:13,422
are based on old Japanese legends and scary stories called kaiaan .

118
00:08:13,547 --> 00:08:17,255
It 's always about grudges , resentment , that kind of thing .

119
00:08:17,380 --> 00:08:21,463
Sorrow , you know ...

120
00:08:21,588 --> 00:08:23,713
Jealousy , sometimes .

121
00:08:23,838 --> 00:08:28,838
So , dead people become these spirits of vengeance ,

122
00:08:28,963 --> 00:08:33,130
and they terrorise the people who are still alive .

123
00:08:33,255 --> 00:08:38,422
Now , I don 't know that I would know how to direct that kind of movie .

124
00:08:38,547 --> 00:08:42,797
I don 't necessarily want to make a movie with that kind of story .

125
00:08:42,922 --> 00:08:46,130
And of course , like we said ,

126
00:08:46,255 --> 00:08:50,047
it didn 't feel like we were making a horror movie .

127
00:08:54,713 --> 00:08:56,505
Yeah , how should I put it ?

128
00:08:56,630 --> 00:09:02,963
Murakami 's original story was about trauma ,

129
00:09:03,088 --> 00:09:07,297
and it seemed to be more of a psychological suspense ,

130
00:09:07,422 --> 00:09:11,838
so this movie was developed with that kind of genre in mind .

131
00:09:11,963 --> 00:09:17,130
I think when people go over the edge , they get totally messed up .

132
00:09:17,255 --> 00:09:20,505
That 's normal . That 's human nature .

133
00:09:20,630 --> 00:09:24,297
And what 's happening isn 't always clear , but using a movie to show that is easier .

134
00:09:24,422 --> 00:09:26,588
We can mix up many things .

135
00:09:27,713 --> 00:09:30,047
Here 's my take on it .

136
00:09:31,422 --> 00:09:35,213
I think that Murakami experienced , in his life ,

137
00:09:35,338 --> 00:09:39,713
a fear similar to what Aoyama ,

138
00:09:39,838 --> 00:09:46,838
played by Ryo Ishibashi , was feeling .

139
00:09:49,088 --> 00:09:54,713
He was making many films at that time , and had many auditions .

140
00:09:54,838 --> 00:09:59,838
In those auditions , I don 't think he met anyone like Asami ,

141
00:09:59,963 --> 00:10:03,130
but he probably met someone ,

142
00:10:03,255 --> 00:10:06,797
and in meeting that person , something strange happened .

143
00:10:06,922 --> 00:10:11,797
I felt like this story could be a personal message to that person .

144
00:10:11,922 --> 00:10:15,838
The message might be " I still love you . "

145
00:10:15,963 --> 00:10:19,463
Pretty unfair form of communication , by the way .

146
00:10:22,463 --> 00:10:26,713
But maybe that 's why it doesn 't seem like fiction .

147
00:10:28,005 --> 00:10:33,838
The original story seems to be written by a writer

148
00:10:33,963 --> 00:10:37,630
who has experienced something like this .

149
00:10:38,922 --> 00:10:43,130
Since Murakami didn 't originally come from the film industry ,

150
00:10:43,255 --> 00:10:46,713
I think that when he got involved in directing and making movies ,

151
00:10:46,838 --> 00:10:50,047
he must have found some things very interesting , even strange .

152
00:10:50,172 --> 00:10:54,588
Like auditions . We do auditions ; that 's what we do .

153
00:10:54,713 --> 00:10:56,963
But from his point of view , he was probably like ,

154
00:10:57,088 --> 00:11:02,630
" Oh , she 's sexy , " and she 's going , " What ? Really ? "

155
00:11:03,838 --> 00:11:08,047
And when he starts wondering why someone would come to that audition ,

156
00:11:08,172 --> 00:11:12,172
that can evolve into a really scary story .

157
00:11:12,297 --> 00:11:16,213
Maybe he did something to someone . We don 't know .

158
00:11:16,338 --> 00:11:21,297
As a writer , he probably saw something

159
00:11:21,422 --> 00:11:24,297
that he found really intriguing .

160
00:11:24,422 --> 00:11:28,547
How was Murakami involved during production ?

161
00:11:28,672 --> 00:11:30,463
Did he show up on the set , or ... ?

162
00:11:30,588 --> 00:11:36,047
No , no . I only met him one time , actually .

163
00:11:39,172 --> 00:11:44,255
We had a meeting and we talked about a lot of things .

164
00:11:44,380 --> 00:11:48,463
He didn 't give me any detailed instructions .

165
00:11:48,588 --> 00:11:53,963
I think he wanted to get a feel for who I was .

166
00:11:54,088 --> 00:12:00,338
He 'd entrusted me with his story ; it 's only natural he 'd want to meet ,

167
00:12:00,463 --> 00:12:06,005
but he didn 't give me any ultimatums or say that I couldn 't do this or that .

168
00:12:06,130 --> 00:12:10,630
He was not that kind of person .

169
00:12:13,130 --> 00:12:16,088
He 's onscreen now .

170
00:12:16,213 --> 00:12:19,505
The main character was played by Ryo Ishibashi .

171
00:12:19,630 --> 00:12:21,547
Ishibashi is also a musician .

172
00:12:21,672 --> 00:12:28,338
He was in a number of Yakuza pictures before Audition , wasn 't he ?

173
00:12:28,463 --> 00:12:33,588
What made you choose him for this role ?

174
00:12:35,130 --> 00:12:39,005
That 's a difficult question .

175
00:12:39,130 --> 00:12:42,088
Well ... we thought ...

176
00:12:45,130 --> 00:12:49,005
It had to be a middle-aged man who was still pretty attractive .

177
00:12:52,547 --> 00:12:55,963
Ten years ago , he looked like

178
00:12:56,088 --> 00:12:59,630
a normal adult man ...

179
00:13:00,630 --> 00:13:03,213
At least to us .

180
00:13:04,380 --> 00:13:09,922
He looked like he was kind to women , and because of that ,

181
00:13:10,047 --> 00:13:16,255
maybe he could be insecure or unhappy even though he 's sexually attractive .

182
00:13:16,380 --> 00:13:19,463
We couldn 't think of anyone else besides Ishibashi .

183
00:13:20,755 --> 00:13:22,838
What else ?

184
00:13:24,297 --> 00:13:27,755
My intuition had told me

185
00:13:27,880 --> 00:13:30,922
that he was hungry for something .

186
00:13:31,047 --> 00:13:35,547
There weren 't many engaging roles in Japanese films at that time .

187
00:13:35,672 --> 00:13:37,797
He was wondering what to do next .

188
00:13:37,922 --> 00:13:43,880
I think he always had Yusaku Matsuda in his mind .

189
00:13:44,005 --> 00:13:47,547
He wanted to be competing with him ,

190
00:13:47,672 --> 00:13:51,047
but there were no good roles for him to compete with Matsuda .

191
00:13:51,172 --> 00:13:57,505
So we thought ... if we can offer him a good movie ,

192
00:13:57,630 --> 00:14:00,380
he could create his own world .

193
00:14:00,505 --> 00:14:03,713
In the scenes toward the end ,

194
00:14:03,838 --> 00:14:06,130
he gets all mangled up .

195
00:14:06,255 --> 00:14:10,422
He can 't even move , and he just groans in pain .

196
00:14:10,547 --> 00:14:15,130
It must have been so uncool for a rocker like him ,

197
00:14:15,255 --> 00:14:20,630
but he wanted the part , he wanted to play it .

198
00:14:20,755 --> 00:14:24,338
It felt right at the time .

199
00:14:27,713 --> 00:14:32,797
How different was the character played by Ishibashi

200
00:14:32,922 --> 00:14:37,047
from the character that Tengan had put in the script ?

201
00:14:37,172 --> 00:14:41,297
It was close .

202
00:14:41,422 --> 00:14:45,338
Like Mike said , he had 10 be sexually attractive .

203
00:14:45,463 --> 00:14:49,088
And he looked indecisive .

204
00:14:49,213 --> 00:14:53,880
In other words , even if he says , " This way , "

205
00:14:54,005 --> 00:14:58,797
he could be thinking , " It may be that way . "

206
00:14:58,922 --> 00:15:02,588
So he 's concerned . He doesn 't know what to do .

207
00:15:02,713 --> 00:15:05,172
He has that kind of feeling .

208
00:15:05,297 --> 00:15:06,297
And ...

209
00:15:07,797 --> 00:15:12,880
his age and his looks matched the character perfectly .

210
00:15:13,005 --> 00:15:15,963
I didn 't know him then .

211
00:15:16,088 --> 00:15:19,922
When I saw him at the screening ,

212
00:15:20,047 --> 00:15:23,755
I asked him to appear in my other movies .

213
00:15:23,880 --> 00:15:27,005
It was because he had made such an impression in this film .

214
00:15:28,338 --> 00:15:31,005
Do you like having auditions

215
00:15:31,130 --> 00:15:33,505
for the films you direct ?

216
00:15:33,630 --> 00:15:34,963
Do you do auditions a lot ?

217
00:15:35,088 --> 00:15:37,213
You mean , like , regular auditions ?

218
00:15:37,338 --> 00:15:38,505
Yes .

219
00:15:38,630 --> 00:15:43,755
Do you ever just pick someone you don 't know to be in your movie ?

220
00:15:45,005 --> 00:15:48,713
Yeah . I think that 's the basis for creating something new .

221
00:15:48,838 --> 00:15:51,713
You might like the person .

222
00:15:52,755 --> 00:15:55,630
It 's a gamble .

223
00:15:55,755 --> 00:15:58,005
You can 't know everything from an audition .

224
00:15:58,505 --> 00:16:01,380
But you can get a feeling .

225
00:16:01,505 --> 00:16:04,463
You chose the person , it 's like trying your luck .

226
00:16:04,588 --> 00:16:07,672
Or , you can insist that you got lucky ,

227
00:16:07,797 --> 00:16:10,922
because you 'd already chosen them long ago .

228
00:16:12,130 --> 00:16:14,213
So ...

229
00:16:14,338 --> 00:16:18,088
But you can 't complain because you 're the one who made the choice .

230
00:16:20,047 --> 00:16:24,213
And sometimes you can 't decide .

231
00:16:24,338 --> 00:16:29,172
How about you , Miike ? Do you like having auditions ?

232
00:16:29,297 --> 00:16:32,672
Yeah , I like them , but ...

233
00:16:32,797 --> 00:16:35,047
when I 'm having an audition ,

234
00:16:35,172 --> 00:16:39,130
I get this funny feeling , like I 'm actually imitating another audition .

235
00:16:39,255 --> 00:16:40,588
In other words ...

236
00:16:41,880 --> 00:16:45,963
I 'm just copying what I 've learned from older colleagues ,

237
00:16:46,088 --> 00:16:50,880
so my auditions aren 't specifically " mine " .

238
00:16:51,005 --> 00:16:56,588
So , these days , my assistant director will hold the auditions ,

239
00:16:56,713 --> 00:17:00,005
which allows me to watch from the side and sort of be more objective .

240
00:17:00,130 --> 00:17:03,922
And if I feel like asking or saying something , I will .

241
00:17:04,047 --> 00:17:08,130
But when I think back , I 've definitely held auditions where ,

242
00:17:08,255 --> 00:17:11,672
the moment the person comes through the door ,

243
00:17:11,797 --> 00:17:13,547
I know they 're the one .

244
00:17:13,672 --> 00:17:15,880
Yeah , me too .

245
00:17:16,005 --> 00:17:20,422
That 's exactly how Aoyama felt in Audition .

246
00:17:20,547 --> 00:17:24,088
He had this strong feeling just from looking at her picture .

247
00:17:24,213 --> 00:17:27,922
So , when he sees her , his mind is already made up .

248
00:17:28,047 --> 00:17:32,005
He asks some questions , but ...

249
00:17:32,588 --> 00:17:36,255
it 's just confirming the feeling he had .

250
00:17:36,380 --> 00:17:39,338
I think it 's very close to my experience .

251
00:17:40,463 --> 00:17:44,838
You encounter someone who was born to play the role .

252
00:17:44,963 --> 00:17:50,172
As soon as the person opens the door , you know that they 're the one .

253
00:17:50,963 --> 00:17:54,005
I think that 's true of a lot of auditions .

254
00:17:55,755 --> 00:17:59,255
How about the auditions for this film ?

255
00:17:59,380 --> 00:18:02,797
That is , for Audition did anything like that happen ?

256
00:18:02,922 --> 00:18:04,422
Do you remember ?

257
00:18:04,547 --> 00:18:07,838
No . For this film , most of the actors were ...

258
00:18:07,963 --> 00:18:12,213
Well , we thought about Asami 's role . We researched a lot .

259
00:18:12,338 --> 00:18:15,963
And we met some people ,

260
00:18:16,088 --> 00:18:19,463
but it wasn 't like an audition .

261
00:18:19,588 --> 00:18:24,255
We already had someone in mind ,

262
00:18:24,380 --> 00:18:29,713
but we thought that maybe that wouldn 't be fair ,

263
00:18:29,838 --> 00:18:33,755
so we decided to see some actors .

264
00:18:33,880 --> 00:18:38,338
For other parts , we sought out ...

265
00:18:38,463 --> 00:18:42,005
unique actors to play certain roles .

266
00:18:43,505 --> 00:18:46,797
This house we used as a set is located in Seijo .

267
00:18:46,922 --> 00:18:51,963
No , it 's located in Denenchofu , an upscale residential area .

268
00:18:52,088 --> 00:18:55,213
We made a surprise visit to this house , which is just owned by a regular family .

269
00:18:55,338 --> 00:18:58,172
We asked them to let us use the house .

270
00:18:58,297 --> 00:19:03,213
We put all the family members in an upstairs room for a few days .

271
00:19:03,338 --> 00:19:06,255
We were filming till midnight .

272
00:19:06,380 --> 00:19:09,005
It 's amazing they let us use the house .

273
00:19:09,130 --> 00:19:11,422
Did you show up there without any notice ?

274
00:19:11,547 --> 00:19:18,130
No , we actually took half a month to negotiate and get consent .

275
00:19:18,255 --> 00:19:22,880
And since we used every room in the house ,

276
00:19:23,005 --> 00:19:25,297
when we left , it was sort of like moving out .

277
00:19:26,338 --> 00:19:29,797
So , you sort of lived on set .

278
00:19:29,922 --> 00:19:33,463
It was a very Japanese way to shoot a movie .

279
00:19:33,588 --> 00:19:37,422
We begged to rent this house from this family .

280
00:19:45,297 --> 00:19:51,047
How did Dead or Alive and Audition influence your career ?

281
00:19:51,172 --> 00:19:54,422
I mean , you used to make direct-to-video movies and genre films .

282
00:19:54,547 --> 00:19:59,172
Now , you make films that are screened at international film festivals .

283
00:19:59,297 --> 00:20:02,588
I think these two films must have given you an opportunity

284
00:20:02,713 --> 00:20:04,380
to go in a new direction .

285
00:20:04,505 --> 00:20:07,797
So , for you , Mike , would you say that 's accurate ,

286
00:20:07,922 --> 00:20:14,047
that these two films had an impact on your life or your career ?

287
00:20:14,172 --> 00:20:20,588
Well , aside from my personal feelings about those two movies ,

288
00:20:20,713 --> 00:20:28,713
the fact is that Audition helped me a lot ,

289
00:20:29,922 --> 00:20:34,380
and it happened at just the right time .

290
00:20:34,505 --> 00:20:37,880
Yes , I directed this movie ,

291
00:20:38,005 --> 00:20:43,172
but without the producers who wanted to make this film

292
00:20:43,297 --> 00:20:46,838
or Murakami 's original story , it wouldn 't have happened .

293
00:20:46,963 --> 00:20:52,047
Without Tengan 's screenplay , it wouldn 't have happened .

294
00:20:52,172 --> 00:20:55,422
I once realised , " I see . "

295
00:20:55,547 --> 00:20:59,047
I am a filmmaker , but it 's not as though

296
00:20:59,172 --> 00:21:02,255
I made the film all by myself .

297
00:21:02,380 --> 00:21:08,130
There were a series of encounters , and this film came along .

298
00:21:08,255 --> 00:21:13,588
And because it did , a lot of things changed significantly .

299
00:21:13,713 --> 00:21:17,755
It started in a small festival called Courmayeur .

300
00:21:17,880 --> 00:21:23,963
From there , it was selected for the Rotterdam Film Festival .

301
00:21:24,755 --> 00:21:27,172
I got an award .

302
00:21:27,297 --> 00:21:31,880
Since I was used to those direct-to-video movies , the " V-Cinema " ,

303
00:21:32,005 --> 00:21:34,463
film festivals were like ...

304
00:21:37,338 --> 00:21:40,797
something from another planet .

305
00:21:42,172 --> 00:21:44,297
So , I thought ...

306
00:21:45,838 --> 00:21:53,047
I wasn 't worried about pleasing foreign audiences .

307
00:21:53,172 --> 00:21:58,838
" If I keep on doing whatever I 'm doing in Japan ,

308
00:21:58,963 --> 00:22:01,005
" people around the world will watch them . "

309
00:22:01,130 --> 00:22:03,963
And that was a great thing .

310
00:22:04,088 --> 00:22:09,297
Film is a very powerful medium .

311
00:22:09,422 --> 00:22:14,297
So , this movie Is very important to me ,

312
00:22:14,422 --> 00:22:18,297
but it 's difficult for me to get away from it .

313
00:22:18,422 --> 00:22:23,088
I 'm currently working on a film that is considered " normal , "

314
00:22:23,213 --> 00:22:27,588
so in Europe they 're going , " Miike is finished , "

315
00:22:27,713 --> 00:22:32,588
and " The man who made Audition is gone . "

316
00:22:39,088 --> 00:22:41,588
But that means that it had a huge impact , didn 't it ?

317
00:22:41,713 --> 00:22:46,338
I think so . After Audition and Visitor O ,

318
00:22:46,463 --> 00:22:51,547
Imprint , which was also written by Tengan , came along ,

319
00:22:51,672 --> 00:22:56,880
and people started to consider me a horror movie director .

320
00:23:01,630 --> 00:23:06,172
We just saw her picture on the screen a little while ago .

321
00:23:06,297 --> 00:23:10,922
Shiina , the main actress , was in Tokyo Gore Police recently .

322
00:23:11,047 --> 00:23:13,380
Right . She 's back . Yes .

323
00:23:14,713 --> 00:23:18,047
She quit once because she had her heart broken .

324
00:23:18,672 --> 00:23:21,922
I heard she was in Kyushu .

325
00:23:22,047 --> 00:23:27,130
She came back and she 's doing good .

326
00:23:27,255 --> 00:23:32,463
I see . So , she hadn 't been doing a lot of acting .

327
00:23:32,588 --> 00:23:34,588
Yeah . Well ...

328
00:23:38,505 --> 00:23:44,880
She doesn 't seem to be interested in living the life of an actress .

329
00:23:45,005 --> 00:23:49,172
She 's had her good moments and her bad moments ,

330
00:23:49,297 --> 00:23:52,130
and , you know ...

331
00:23:52,255 --> 00:23:55,713
It 's not everything . It 's not the most important thing .

332
00:23:55,838 --> 00:23:58,422
She started out as a model .

333
00:23:59,630 --> 00:24:02,505
She was different from other actresses .

334
00:24:02,630 --> 00:24:08,963
The feeling she gives this film wasn 't created from whole cloth .

335
00:24:09,088 --> 00:24:13,963
It was kind of her true colour .

336
00:24:14,088 --> 00:24:17,130
We wanted to bring that feeling , as it was , into the film .

337
00:24:17,255 --> 00:24:19,463
That 's Eihi Shiina .

338
00:24:20,380 --> 00:24:24,713
It 's difficult to create that kind of aura in acting .

339
00:24:24,838 --> 00:24:28,047
We have to make up an episode for her .

340
00:24:28,172 --> 00:24:32,588
There isn 't much information about her in the movie ,

341
00:24:32,713 --> 00:24:36,922
but her presence itself just feels ominous and foreboding .

342
00:24:38,713 --> 00:24:42,047
How about you , Tengan ?

343
00:24:42,172 --> 00:24:48,880
Yeah , she 's very mysterious .

344
00:24:49,005 --> 00:24:54,047
Wasn 't she in a Yukisada movie ? I saw her in something at a screening .

345
00:24:55,172 --> 00:25:01,005
She was mysterious . We 're not sure if she 's like that normally .

346
00:25:01,130 --> 00:25:05,588
And I think we didn 't really get to know her .

347
00:25:05,713 --> 00:25:09,422
We wanted to keep our distance to keep that feeling as real as possible .

348
00:25:10,172 --> 00:25:13,755
It feels like she has something inside that is disturbing .

349
00:25:13,880 --> 00:25:18,755
Her natural tension just makes people feel that way .

350
00:25:18,880 --> 00:25:21,422
I think that fit the film .

351
00:25:23,255 --> 00:25:25,713
Now we 're seeing the audition scene of this film .

352
00:25:25,838 --> 00:25:27,713
Who are these people ?

353
00:25:29,838 --> 00:25:33,713
Some of them are young prospective actresses .

354
00:25:33,838 --> 00:25:37,713
By the way , this lady is a staff member .

355
00:25:38,838 --> 00:25:44,047
We improvised some of these , which made it like a real audition .

356
00:25:44,172 --> 00:25:47,880
We just left the camera rolling and kept the good ones .

357
00:25:48,005 --> 00:25:52,338
But I 'm not really very good at that kind of thing .

358
00:25:52,463 --> 00:25:56,505
It could 've been better .

359
00:25:56,630 --> 00:25:58,838
This scene is kind of sluggish .

360
00:26:00,963 --> 00:26:04,255
But the sluggishness could be perceived as being very Japanese .

361
00:26:05,880 --> 00:26:11,505
In Japan , once we say " Okay , " it 's difficult to do it again .

362
00:26:12,797 --> 00:26:19,297
Back then , it was very difficult to check rushes and reshoot the scene .

363
00:26:19,422 --> 00:26:23,713
You 're destined to make some mistakes .

364
00:26:23,838 --> 00:26:26,880
Your film will have some mistakes .

365
00:26:28,172 --> 00:26:32,047
Accept it and move on .

366
00:26:32,172 --> 00:26:39,213
So , that brings some uncertainty and imperfections that were not planned ,

367
00:26:39,338 --> 00:26:42,422
but the effect is mostly positive , I think .

368
00:26:42,547 --> 00:26:47,255
If it was perfect , and it dictated the audience 's feeling ,

369
00:26:47,380 --> 00:26:50,838
people would feel like it was too calculated and overdone ,

370
00:26:50,963 --> 00:26:55,838
and I 'd guess it probably wouldn 't have been so popular .

371
00:26:58,505 --> 00:27:03,255
It looks like you took a long time to shoot this scene .

372
00:27:03,380 --> 00:27:06,963
It was just rolling .

373
00:27:07,088 --> 00:27:10,297
There were no bloopers .

374
00:27:10,422 --> 00:27:15,838
We would just call " Next ! " That 's all we did .

375
00:27:15,963 --> 00:27:22,672
And there were people who were watching the audition .

376
00:27:22,797 --> 00:27:26,005
They were waiting for their turn and watching on the side .

377
00:27:26,130 --> 00:27:30,005
They watched the people performing .

378
00:27:30,130 --> 00:27:34,505
They couldn 't figure out what to do . Some tried very hard .

379
00:27:35,088 --> 00:27:39,505
Others were totally nervous .

380
00:27:39,630 --> 00:27:44,755
There were no directions , so it was very realistic .

381
00:27:48,130 --> 00:27:51,297
Even just looking at her back , I feel something scary .

382
00:27:51,422 --> 00:27:57,713
She has something . I wonder how she is in real life .

383
00:27:59,630 --> 00:28:02,338
We 're going to see some horrific stuff .

384
00:28:02,880 --> 00:28:07,255
If we could see her ambition as an actress or something ,

385
00:28:07,380 --> 00:28:13,797
we could understand what she wanted to do .

386
00:28:13,922 --> 00:28:18,755
But she wasn 't like that . She was so mysterious .

387
00:28:18,880 --> 00:28:22,713
We didn 't want to ruin the mystery ,

388
00:28:22,838 --> 00:28:26,088
so we tried not to talk to her much .

389
00:28:26,213 --> 00:28:31,005
We didn 't try to understand her , and she didn 't ask us anything .

390
00:28:31,130 --> 00:28:33,588
That was our relationship .

391
00:28:33,713 --> 00:28:36,088
Even 10 years later ,

392
00:28:36,213 --> 00:28:41,922
I probably still don 't know who she is .

393
00:28:56,088 --> 00:28:59,005
By the way , this scene is too long .

394
00:29:00,963 --> 00:29:06,130
We wanted to show the audition going on and on and on

395
00:29:06,255 --> 00:29:10,880
to show how long it took to finally get to her .

396
00:29:13,213 --> 00:29:20,922
The title is Audition and this is the only audition scene .

397
00:29:25,630 --> 00:29:31,005
I think these two male characters are totally opposite .

398
00:29:36,713 --> 00:29:39,088
Kunimura is ...

399
00:29:42,422 --> 00:29:44,172
How can I put this ?

400
00:29:44,297 --> 00:29:49,963
Back then , he was already established , but he was an unusual type .

401
00:29:51,130 --> 00:29:57,713
He wasn 't an actor everybody knew .

402
00:29:59,963 --> 00:30:05,130
The pros knew who he was .

403
00:30:05,255 --> 00:30:10,505
He was very cooperative .

404
00:30:10,630 --> 00:30:16,172
He doesn 't have much ego , and he pays attention to what 's going on .

405
00:30:16,297 --> 00:30:19,755
He thinks about how his character should be played

406
00:30:19,880 --> 00:30:22,630
in relation to the other character , Aoyama , played by Ishibashi .

407
00:30:22,755 --> 00:30:28,172
Of course , it was described in the script , but for example ,

408
00:30:28,297 --> 00:30:32,213
he chooses his tone of voice based on the other actors .

409
00:30:32,338 --> 00:30:38,130
It depended on how the others acted .

410
00:30:38,255 --> 00:30:43,130
He is a wonderful actor for a director .

411
00:30:47,213 --> 00:30:51,838
In this scene , she is wearing a white dress .

412
00:30:51,963 --> 00:30:53,380
Does that mean something ?

413
00:30:53,505 --> 00:30:57,588
It 's more of a suit .

414
00:30:57,713 --> 00:30:59,463
Well ...

415
00:31:01,047 --> 00:31:06,755
We wanted it as simple as possible .

416
00:31:08,088 --> 00:31:11,297
It doesn 't show much pride .

417
00:31:13,380 --> 00:31:18,547
At first glance , she looks like an open person .

418
00:31:18,672 --> 00:31:25,463
But you can 't actually tell who she is .

419
00:31:27,922 --> 00:31:35,047
We tried to use a simple costume for her .

420
00:31:35,172 --> 00:31:40,172
It applies to her hairstyle and makeup . She had very little makeup on .

421
00:31:44,172 --> 00:31:46,713
How old was she back then ?

422
00:31:46,838 --> 00:31:48,172
Who ? Shiina ?

423
00:31:48,297 --> 00:31:54,547
I think she was 23 or 24 .

424
00:32:03,297 --> 00:32:06,838
Ishibashi is talking now .

425
00:32:16,005 --> 00:32:19,422
She has an unusual face .

426
00:32:28,588 --> 00:32:32,172
Let 's talk about Tengan 's career .

427
00:32:32,297 --> 00:32:33,505
Okay .

428
00:32:33,630 --> 00:32:38,005
You 've been involved In many movies as a writer ,

429
00:32:38,130 --> 00:32:41,172
and you direct , too .

430
00:32:41,297 --> 00:32:44,297
Can we talk about the films you directed ?

431
00:32:46,922 --> 00:32:50,422
- The films I directed ? - Yeah .

432
00:32:50,547 --> 00:32:58,255
I directed Aiki and Ishibashi was in that , too .

433
00:32:58,380 --> 00:33:01,547
And I did Waiting in the Dark .

434
00:33:01,672 --> 00:33:05,505
The Most Beautiful Night in the World hit the screens last year .

435
00:33:05,630 --> 00:33:11,380
I 've done some documentaries , like Muteki no Handicap .

436
00:33:12,338 --> 00:33:14,380
I 'm so twisted .

437
00:33:16,630 --> 00:33:19,630
I 'm twisted .

438
00:33:19,755 --> 00:33:23,422
Last year 's movie was so twisted .

439
00:33:26,255 --> 00:33:31,255
- What do you think ? - I 'm a huge fan .

440
00:33:31,380 --> 00:33:34,922
He 's not trying to please people .

441
00:33:35,047 --> 00:33:39,755
I think , in Japan ,

442
00:33:39,880 --> 00:33:45,380
there are very few people who make films like that .

443
00:33:47,422 --> 00:33:50,630
It 's like it 's not his job .

444
00:33:51,672 --> 00:33:58,547
It 's more like fate . He makes films because it 's his fate .

445
00:33:58,672 --> 00:34:03,755
Meanwhile , we make films to make a living .

446
00:34:03,880 --> 00:34:06,005
It 's the complete opposite .

447
00:34:07,463 --> 00:34:09,380
What do you think ?

448
00:34:09,505 --> 00:34:15,172
Maybe it 's not my job . Maybe it 's fate , like you say .

449
00:34:17,713 --> 00:34:19,172
That 's the ideal .

450
00:34:19,297 --> 00:34:23,713
If it were like that forever that 'd be great ,

451
00:34:23,838 --> 00:34:26,213
but it can 't always be like that .

452
00:34:28,547 --> 00:34:33,755
Did you like writing Audition ?

453
00:34:33,880 --> 00:34:37,047
Yeah . I like writing screenplays .

454
00:34:37,172 --> 00:34:42,838
So , I enjoy writing for myself and other people .

455
00:34:42,963 --> 00:34:47,547
I really enjoy writing .

456
00:34:48,547 --> 00:34:50,630
Is there a difference

457
00:34:50,755 --> 00:34:54,172
between writing for yourself and writing for other directors ?

458
00:34:54,297 --> 00:34:58,880
Recently , there has not been much difference .

459
00:35:00,213 --> 00:35:04,838
But when I 've never worked with a particular director ,

460
00:35:04,963 --> 00:35:08,547
since I don 't know the person very well ,

461
00:35:08,672 --> 00:35:11,797
I 'll tend to write more details .

462
00:35:11,922 --> 00:35:16,005
This was the first film that I worked on with Miike .

463
00:35:16,130 --> 00:35:21,338
The whole time I was writing , I wondered how Miike would direct it .

464
00:35:21,463 --> 00:35:25,005
The script was very detailed , so I had a lot of questions .

465
00:35:26,463 --> 00:35:31,672
" What does that mean ? " " When did this happen ? "

466
00:35:33,047 --> 00:35:38,005
But everything just had to be accepted .

467
00:35:38,130 --> 00:35:44,297
I realised that maybe I didn 't have to understand everything .

468
00:35:46,130 --> 00:35:50,213
You can 't always tell which scene is from whose point of view .

469
00:35:50,338 --> 00:35:53,588
When I was reading this story ,

470
00:35:53,713 --> 00:35:59,922
I thought everybody would share time with somebody ,

471
00:36:00,047 --> 00:36:05,630
but each person probably has a totally individual perspective .

472
00:36:05,755 --> 00:36:10,630
Three people are here right now , but we all see this moment differently ,

473
00:36:10,755 --> 00:36:14,172
and things take on entirely different meanings from alternate points of view .

474
00:36:14,297 --> 00:36:19,213
That 's the reality . When we fall in love and spend time together ,

475
00:36:19,338 --> 00:36:23,880
we tend to think we share the same time and same kinds of feelings ,

476
00:36:24,005 --> 00:36:28,755
but actually there are two thoroughly different worlds .

477
00:36:28,880 --> 00:36:34,963
Each person has his or her own world .

478
00:36:35,088 --> 00:36:41,088
And people forget to think of each other 's feelings , or world .

479
00:36:41,213 --> 00:36:45,380
So , oddly ...

480
00:36:45,505 --> 00:36:51,463
the sequence in the last half was actually kind of natural .

481
00:36:51,588 --> 00:36:54,505
I wasn 't trying to make it eccentric .

482
00:36:54,630 --> 00:36:58,713
I shot it in a natural way .

483
00:37:02,463 --> 00:37:07,172
So , after Audition , as you guys told me ,

484
00:37:07,297 --> 00:37:13,130
you worked together on Imprint for Showtime 's Masters of Horror .

485
00:37:13,255 --> 00:37:16,172
I was criticised by so many people for that .

486
00:37:16,755 --> 00:37:19,088
I was told to do whatever I wanted to do .

487
00:37:19,213 --> 00:37:27,463
American producers watched Audition , and they liked it .

488
00:37:27,588 --> 00:37:32,838
I was chosen for Masters of Horror - All big-name directors on the series .

489
00:37:32,963 --> 00:37:37,005
And the 13th film , the last in the series , was mine .

490
00:37:37,130 --> 00:37:41,838
I wondered why they chose mine for last .

491
00:37:41,963 --> 00:37:47,630
I thought , " This is going to be so much fun . "

492
00:37:47,755 --> 00:37:51,255
It was for cable TV , and they said I could do whatever I wanted .

493
00:37:51,380 --> 00:37:55,672
America is a country of liberty . There is freedom of expression .

494
00:37:55,797 --> 00:37:59,005
They told me to do something I couldn 't get away with in Japan .

495
00:37:59,130 --> 00:38:01,880
So I did it , and they said , " That 's too much . "

496
00:38:02,005 --> 00:38:05,672
They said , " We can 't air this on cable TV . "

497
00:38:05,797 --> 00:38:11,297
In England , it was shown uncut ,

498
00:38:11,422 --> 00:38:16,880
so it was still watched by a lot of people .

499
00:38:17,005 --> 00:38:21,005
But I do think that putting Tengan and I together ...

500
00:38:22,755 --> 00:38:26,338
IS dangerous for producers .

501
00:38:26,463 --> 00:38:34,338
But we haven 't learned our lesson . He 's writing a story for me right now .

502
00:38:34,463 --> 00:38:38,838
It 's going to be a serious , period-piece drama ,

503
00:38:38,963 --> 00:38:43,005
and Tengan is writing the story .

504
00:38:43,130 --> 00:38:46,630
- Is it 13 Assassins ? - Yes .

505
00:38:46,755 --> 00:38:50,172
I think some folks in Toho may even pass out ...

506
00:38:51,505 --> 00:38:53,255
when they learn what the story is about .

507
00:38:53,380 --> 00:38:56,172
They don 't understand us .

508
00:38:56,297 --> 00:38:58,130
It 's unbelievable .

509
00:38:58,255 --> 00:39:03,797
Yeah , they 'll misunderstand . They 'll never get it .

510
00:39:07,297 --> 00:39:10,213
So , are you allowed to talk about the story ?

511
00:39:10,338 --> 00:39:14,588
It 's not that I 'm not allowed . It 's just that it 's unfinished .

512
00:39:14,713 --> 00:39:19,630
We don 't even know where the story will end up yet .

513
00:39:19,755 --> 00:39:24,630
I can understand distributors worrying . We need a big audience .

514
00:39:24,755 --> 00:39:27,755
And it 's going to cost a lot ,

515
00:39:27,880 --> 00:39:30,797
so if we don 't get a big audience

516
00:39:30,922 --> 00:39:33,797
we will lose money , and I understand that .

517
00:39:34,797 --> 00:39:41,463
But how do we want to entertain the audience ?

518
00:39:41,588 --> 00:39:46,797
Maybe it 'll just be an okay film for people to come and have a good time ,

519
00:39:46,922 --> 00:39:53,380
but that 's not what we 're after , that 's not what we 're shooting for .

520
00:39:53,505 --> 00:39:59,380
We don 't find that kind of movie very attractive .

521
00:39:59,505 --> 00:40:05,297
We 're not against that kind of thing ,

522
00:40:05,422 --> 00:40:10,922
but if we 're being true to ourselves , the movie is going to be crazy .

523
00:40:11,047 --> 00:40:16,630
There are landmines placed throughout the story .

524
00:40:16,755 --> 00:40:22,172
It 's very exciting .

525
00:40:22,297 --> 00:40:28,838
We 're going to set a new record for most people killed in a story .

526
00:40:31,297 --> 00:40:37,297
The number is unbelievable .

527
00:40:37,422 --> 00:40:40,713
It seems like no matter what I write , there are always characters left to Kill .

528
00:40:40,838 --> 00:40:43,213
Just wait until you see it .

529
00:40:44,755 --> 00:40:46,880
So , the screenplay is almost finished ?

530
00:40:47,005 --> 00:40:50,963
Yes , almost .

531
00:40:51,088 --> 00:40:53,672
Are you planning on shooting this year ?

532
00:40:53,797 --> 00:40:56,297
Yes .

533
00:40:56,422 --> 00:41:04,588
We are planning to show it at the Venice Film Festival next year .

534
00:41:05,630 --> 00:41:07,630
That 's our goal .

535
00:41:10,005 --> 00:41:15,630
Mike , you studied filmmaking at a school

536
00:41:15,755 --> 00:41:20,588
founded by Tengan 's father , Shohei Imamura , right ?

537
00:41:20,713 --> 00:41:24,255
Well , I went to school , but I 'm not sure if I studied or not !

538
00:41:24,380 --> 00:41:32,130
I would like to ask about your relationship with Imamura .

539
00:41:32,255 --> 00:41:37,922
Can you tell me about your work relationship ?

540
00:41:38,047 --> 00:41:40,088
Or were you friends ?

541
00:41:40,213 --> 00:41:45,172
Well , I was ...

542
00:41:45,297 --> 00:41:49,463
before ... how do I put this ?

543
00:41:52,297 --> 00:41:55,880
Imamura is surrounded by fascinating people ,

544
00:41:56,005 --> 00:41:59,422
a lot of very unique folks .

545
00:41:59,547 --> 00:42:03,880
And you go , " How did these people get involved in filmmaking ? "

546
00:42:04,005 --> 00:42:09,213
I was working as an assistant director for TV shows .

547
00:42:09,797 --> 00:42:15,255
My role was to make sure the production was on track .

548
00:42:15,380 --> 00:42:20,463
The job and I were like oil and water .

549
00:42:20,588 --> 00:42:24,422
I don 't think I 'm someone who should ever be an assistant director .

550
00:42:24,547 --> 00:42:29,422
A person like me is not supposed to be there , at least usually .

551
00:42:29,547 --> 00:42:33,797
But I guess I was useful in a way .

552
00:42:33,922 --> 00:42:40,588
I don 't think Imamura was expecting much from me

553
00:42:40,713 --> 00:42:42,547
as an assistant director .

554
00:42:42,672 --> 00:42:46,922
I wasn 't Imamura 's understudy or anything like that .

555
00:42:47,047 --> 00:42:52,713
The way they lived and worked was reflected in their movies .

556
00:42:52,838 --> 00:42:57,672
It was about how your life became intertwined with filmmaking .

557
00:42:57,797 --> 00:43:01,588
That was the big difference from other directors ,

558
00:43:01,713 --> 00:43:05,422
and that 's not something I can duplicate .

559
00:43:05,547 --> 00:43:14,255
So , I have to make films in my way and with my own voice .

560
00:43:14,380 --> 00:43:18,463
I just have to do it in my own style .

561
00:43:18,588 --> 00:43:24,463
That 's what I learned from him . In that sense , I 'm like him .

562
00:43:24,588 --> 00:43:27,255
Doing something different from him was the way to go .

563
00:43:28,505 --> 00:43:33,630
But I really regret that I wasn 't of much help to him .

564
00:43:35,713 --> 00:43:41,755
He had this way of looking at me , like , " Why are you here ? "

565
00:43:43,630 --> 00:43:47,838
So , did you guys know each other before this ?

566
00:43:47,963 --> 00:43:52,172
Not really . We had only met once .

567
00:43:52,297 --> 00:43:56,963
You might not remember this , but I met you at a New Year 's party .

568
00:43:57,088 --> 00:43:59,255
Yeah , that 's right .

569
00:43:59,380 --> 00:44:01,713
At that time ...

570
00:44:03,338 --> 00:44:08,630
I had just started working for Imamura .

571
00:44:12,130 --> 00:44:14,213
I see .

572
00:44:14,338 --> 00:44:19,255
So , we knew each other but hadn 't yet had a conversation ,

573
00:44:19,380 --> 00:44:23,172
and I got a call from him for this film .

574
00:44:23,297 --> 00:44:28,588
I heard about him through Inagaki ,

575
00:44:28,713 --> 00:44:32,630
who was working for Imamura as a set designer .

576
00:44:34,463 --> 00:44:40,838
They were saying , " Daisuke Tengan Is a really thought-provoking guy . "

577
00:44:40,963 --> 00:44:45,755
The people talking about Tengan 's productions really seemed to ...

578
00:44:47,838 --> 00:44:50,505
enjoy feeling the pain .

579
00:44:50,630 --> 00:44:54,547
I think they really felt like they were making something .

580
00:44:54,672 --> 00:44:57,463
They seemed to have fun and enjoy the uniqueness .

581
00:44:57,588 --> 00:45:00,422
It had obviously been a pleasant experience .

582
00:45:04,880 --> 00:45:06,838
Is that accurate ?

583
00:45:06,963 --> 00:45:09,922
Yeah , my productions are tough ,

584
00:45:10,047 --> 00:45:13,172
but I think Miike 's productions are tough , too .

585
00:45:13,297 --> 00:45:15,922
Yes , physically tough .

586
00:45:16,047 --> 00:45:18,963
It 's the same thing .

587
00:45:20,005 --> 00:45:26,547
We didn 't have a lot of time to shoot this , you know ?

588
00:45:26,672 --> 00:45:28,255
How many days ?

589
00:45:28,380 --> 00:45:32,630
How many days ? I don 't remember ...

590
00:45:32,755 --> 00:45:38,297
but I think it was about three weeks .

591
00:45:41,588 --> 00:45:43,422
Is that too short for you now ?

592
00:45:43,547 --> 00:45:50,880
No , but back then , three weeks was one week longer than usual .

593
00:45:51,005 --> 00:45:55,213
On most of my movies , I had to shoot everything in two weeks ,

594
00:45:55,338 --> 00:45:59,797
and I got really used to working like that .

595
00:45:59,922 --> 00:46:04,547
I knew how to direct in that kind of schedule .

596
00:46:04,672 --> 00:46:10,005
As time went on , I 'd get more and more days to shoot .

597
00:46:10,130 --> 00:46:17,088
If , all of a sudden , I get a whole three months ,

598
00:46:17,213 --> 00:46:20,047
that would be a huge movie .

599
00:46:20,172 --> 00:46:22,380
If we worked for three months

600
00:46:22,505 --> 00:46:25,713
as intensely as we work on those two-week shoots , we 'd die .

601
00:46:25,838 --> 00:46:30,672
So , having three months means we 'd work more leisurely .

602
00:46:30,797 --> 00:46:35,088
We 'd have to make each day a good deal easier on everyone .

603
00:46:35,213 --> 00:46:39,255
I didn 't know how to do that .

604
00:46:39,380 --> 00:46:41,047
Far from it ,

605
00:46:41,172 --> 00:46:47,963
my style was pushing everyone as much as I could , working hard ,

606
00:46:48,088 --> 00:46:55,213
even when we were exhausted , and finishing in two or three weeks .

607
00:46:56,797 --> 00:47:02,005
Tengan , what about you ? Was it always a battle against time ?

608
00:47:02,130 --> 00:47:05,338
Yeah , it was like that for me , too .

609
00:47:05,463 --> 00:47:10,005
Short and concentrated .

610
00:47:10,130 --> 00:47:15,255
Even if we have a lot of days to shoot , it still feels very tight .

611
00:47:15,380 --> 00:47:17,838
You have to struggle to keep your concentration .

612
00:47:23,755 --> 00:47:29,338
I heard Tarantino talking about you .

613
00:47:29,463 --> 00:47:34,588
He said Miike was the director who was making the most films .

614
00:47:34,713 --> 00:47:37,547
And it 's true . You make a lot of movies .

615
00:47:37,672 --> 00:47:42,797
What 's that like ? How are you able to do so many ?

616
00:47:42,922 --> 00:47:46,963
Well , there are a lot of directors who are just constantly putting out films .

617
00:47:47,088 --> 00:47:53,047
Directors of " Pink Films " in Japan , for instance , put out tons of stuff .

618
00:47:53,172 --> 00:47:56,630
In Mexico , there are movies coming out all the time .

619
00:47:56,755 --> 00:48:01,297
It was never my intention , really ,

620
00:48:01,422 --> 00:48:06,505
to be known for making a lot of films .

621
00:48:06,630 --> 00:48:09,547
But it 's kind of convenient .

622
00:48:10,838 --> 00:48:13,505
Oh , that 's scary . The bag moved suddenly .

623
00:48:15,422 --> 00:48:18,463
It just kind of happened .

624
00:48:18,588 --> 00:48:27,672
I didn 't set out to make so many . It wasn 't part of some big scheme .

625
00:48:27,797 --> 00:48:32,880
It just kind of happened that way .

626
00:48:33,005 --> 00:48:40,172
After every movie , I always feel like I only have one movie left in me .

627
00:48:40,297 --> 00:48:43,255
When I 'm shooting a longer picture ,

628
00:48:43,380 --> 00:48:46,922
I think about making commercials or something on the side ,

629
00:48:47,047 --> 00:48:50,255
just by using extra time , the crew and equipment are already there .

630
00:48:50,380 --> 00:48:55,005
I don 't think it 's necessarily a good thing to make so many movies ...

631
00:48:58,130 --> 00:49:04,338
but in Japan you can 't really make a living if you 're not shooting all the time .

632
00:49:11,463 --> 00:49:13,130
I understand .

633
00:49:24,088 --> 00:49:27,338
Ishibashi looked skinnier back then .

634
00:49:27,463 --> 00:49:31,297
I think he 's a size bigger now . He looks very dignified .

635
00:49:34,588 --> 00:49:39,713
He didn 't always have a lot of roles , but things have been happening .

636
00:49:41,213 --> 00:49:46,213
He had always wanted ...

637
00:49:48,713 --> 00:49:52,838
to work in Hollywood , to try something ...

638
00:49:54,630 --> 00:49:58,172
he wanted to try something outside of Japan .

639
00:49:58,297 --> 00:50:03,713
And if he wants to follow Matsuda 's example ,

640
00:50:03,838 --> 00:50:08,880
he has to go abroad , and that 's what he 's been doing .

641
00:50:09,005 --> 00:50:14,588
He 's been putting himself out there . He learned English .

642
00:50:22,922 --> 00:50:26,463
Miike , did you go through any changes

643
00:50:26,588 --> 00:50:30,088
during the shooting of this ?

644
00:50:31,922 --> 00:50:36,547
Well ... how can I put this ?

645
00:50:38,630 --> 00:50:40,380
Well ...

646
00:50:42,880 --> 00:50:46,672
This was not the first one .

647
00:50:46,797 --> 00:50:53,755
Even though it wasn 't a big production , I wanted to use film ,

648
00:50:53,880 --> 00:50:56,880
and I wanted to make it for theatrical release .

649
00:50:57,005 --> 00:51:03,213
Before this , I used film for promotion ,

650
00:51:03,338 --> 00:51:08,047
to sell videos to the theatre audiences ,

651
00:51:08,172 --> 00:51:12,297
theatrical films as promotion ,

652
00:51:12,422 --> 00:51:17,380
so even though this wasn 't going to have a big budget ,

653
00:51:17,505 --> 00:51:23,380
it was my intention for it to be seen theatrically .

654
00:51:23,505 --> 00:51:31,005
It was really the first film that I made specifically for that format .

655
00:51:38,172 --> 00:51:41,880
You used to make " V-Cinema " ,

656
00:51:42,005 --> 00:51:48,838
but after Audition and Dead or Alive

657
00:51:48,963 --> 00:51:56,047
your area was bigger , your audience was bigger .

658
00:51:56,172 --> 00:52:01,213
You made bigger pictures ,

659
00:52:01,338 --> 00:52:05,713
so you sort of went through three different arenas of film .

660
00:52:07,172 --> 00:52:10,338
Now do you think about the budget or the genre

661
00:52:10,463 --> 00:52:14,172
when you are considering a project

662
00:52:14,297 --> 00:52:18,213
and thinking about which gig to accept ?

663
00:52:18,338 --> 00:52:21,088
No . No .

664
00:52:22,630 --> 00:52:27,422
Usually ... the way it usually goes ...

665
00:52:29,922 --> 00:52:33,172
If I 'm not paying close enough attention , then it can really go any way .

666
00:52:33,297 --> 00:52:39,755
But from the producers that I used to work with ,

667
00:52:39,880 --> 00:52:43,047
to just the people I hung out with ,

668
00:52:43,172 --> 00:52:48,088
some , obviously , have come and gone over the years ,

669
00:52:48,213 --> 00:52:50,630
but others have kept plugging away .

670
00:52:50,755 --> 00:52:53,380
Sometimes people will form a new company or something ,

671
00:52:53,505 --> 00:52:56,047
and that might be a place where I can bring a project .

672
00:52:56,172 --> 00:53:00,338
Time passes , and gradually ...

673
00:53:00,463 --> 00:53:04,255
everyone who once worked together has scattered to different areas ,

674
00:53:04,380 --> 00:53:07,713
and each person is working hard to make their own way .

675
00:53:07,838 --> 00:53:11,213
But when they get the chance ,

676
00:53:11,338 --> 00:53:17,088
sometimes they 'll think of me and give me a call .

677
00:53:17,213 --> 00:53:25,297
This has happened time and time again with people I used to know .

678
00:53:25,422 --> 00:53:33,130
Inevitably , my films are staffed with folks who 've all worked with me before .

679
00:53:36,130 --> 00:53:38,047
I see .

680
00:53:38,172 --> 00:53:45,797
My goal isn 't necessarily to get every person in the country to see it ,

681
00:53:45,922 --> 00:53:49,963
but when a lot of people watch your movie

682
00:53:50,088 --> 00:53:54,213
and you 're recognised in the industry ,

683
00:53:54,338 --> 00:54:01,172
it 's easier to get a lot more freedom on these low-budget projects .

684
00:54:01,297 --> 00:54:04,338
Casting becomes easier .

685
00:54:04,463 --> 00:54:07,255
You don 't have to sacrifice quality .

686
00:54:07,380 --> 00:54:12,213
So , I will keep on making films and meeting more people .

687
00:54:12,338 --> 00:54:18,797
Look at stuff like genre movies or " Pink Films " ,

688
00:54:18,922 --> 00:54:23,005
those can be made with very low budgets .

689
00:54:23,838 --> 00:54:26,088
They don 't have to be huge movies .

690
00:54:26,213 --> 00:54:31,838
I want to make interesting movies .

691
00:54:34,713 --> 00:54:39,047
What are your plans for the future , Tengan ?

692
00:54:39,172 --> 00:54:40,588
More directing ?

693
00:54:40,713 --> 00:54:42,422
Well ...

694
00:54:43,630 --> 00:54:52,005
My approach to filmmaking is a little different from Miike 's .

695
00:54:55,963 --> 00:55:01,130
When a professional makes a film , it can be intriguing ,

696
00:55:01,255 --> 00:55:05,463
but lately I 'm thinking that there are limits to what they can do .

697
00:55:05,588 --> 00:55:10,422
I want to make something that is amateurish .

698
00:55:10,547 --> 00:55:16,047
A pro makes movies as an occupation .

699
00:55:16,172 --> 00:55:18,755
They don 't want anything in there to be unnecessary ,

700
00:55:18,880 --> 00:55:22,630
but they lose something when they always work in that mind-set .

701
00:55:22,755 --> 00:55:25,880
At least , that 's how I feel .

702
00:55:29,297 --> 00:55:35,963
It 's kind of the opposite of how big movies are approached .

703
00:55:37,838 --> 00:55:42,588
My roots are in independent film ,

704
00:55:42,713 --> 00:55:50,255
and I think that 's the direction I want to return to now .

705
00:55:54,047 --> 00:55:58,213
It 's difficult in Japan , because there aren 't as many producers .

706
00:56:00,547 --> 00:56:02,963
I mean ...

707
00:56:04,505 --> 00:56:09,922
I find something interesting and want to do it with these people ,

708
00:56:10,047 --> 00:56:14,172
and we find some people to make it happen .

709
00:56:14,297 --> 00:56:19,255
Then we get together and create something .

710
00:56:19,380 --> 00:56:26,963
But the current producers in Japan are kind of all the same ; they 're trendy .

711
00:56:27,088 --> 00:56:33,047
They say , when we edit , " It 's too long . "

712
00:56:33,172 --> 00:56:35,922
And I think , " What 's wrong with being too long ? "

713
00:56:36,047 --> 00:56:41,630
They say , " The pace isn 't good , " or " This line isn 't necessary . "

714
00:56:41,755 --> 00:56:46,880
They don 't want anything unnecessary .

715
00:56:48,047 --> 00:56:53,755
If the movie 's nothing but good stuff , I think that gets kind of boring .

716
00:56:55,005 --> 00:56:58,463
They like big stars in the movies ,

717
00:56:58,588 --> 00:57:00,963
and the big stars have to look cool .

718
00:57:01,088 --> 00:57:04,255
And to make them look cool , they bring in other , less-cool actors .

719
00:57:04,380 --> 00:57:07,380
It 's so unnatural .

720
00:57:07,505 --> 00:57:11,255
People who try to resist that tendency ,

721
00:57:11,380 --> 00:57:15,005
I think that 's a great quality to have .

722
00:57:15,130 --> 00:57:20,672
That 's definitely something Tengan has .

723
00:57:20,797 --> 00:57:25,213
It 's a weapon that you can use to shape your film .

724
00:57:25,338 --> 00:57:31,172
In America , there are factors that create variety in filmmaking .

725
00:57:31,297 --> 00:57:35,505
For example , we can 't tell if Steven Soderbergh is a pro or not .

726
00:57:35,630 --> 00:57:38,922
Tarantino is totally an amateur ...

727
00:57:39,047 --> 00:57:40,922
at least in that sense .

728
00:57:41,047 --> 00:57:43,713
Can you explain that ?

729
00:57:43,838 --> 00:57:46,338
He doesn 't know how to shoot .

730
00:57:46,463 --> 00:57:47,547
Really ?

731
00:57:47,672 --> 00:57:51,422
He knows what kind of shot he wants .

732
00:57:51,547 --> 00:57:54,630
When I went to see the shooting of Grindhouse ,

733
00:57:54,755 --> 00:57:57,713
he was having fun riding in the stunt car .

734
00:57:57,838 --> 00:58:00,422
He was saying , " This is scary ! It 's so cooll "

735
00:58:00,547 --> 00:58:04,005
And they just keep on going . They go on and on .

736
00:58:04,130 --> 00:58:09,130
In that sense , he 's not trying to be a " professional " filmmaker .

737
00:58:09,255 --> 00:58:13,463
And for the audience , that 's why he 's great ,

738
00:58:13,588 --> 00:58:18,922
they keep that atmosphere and let him make whatever he wants .

739
00:58:19,047 --> 00:58:24,213
They can still commercialise his films and make a lot of money .

740
00:58:24,338 --> 00:58:29,213
But there are some people who are really proactive .

741
00:58:29,338 --> 00:58:31,547
They challenge .

742
00:58:31,672 --> 00:58:36,713
They want to be successful , even for just a short time , in Hollywood ,

743
00:58:36,838 --> 00:58:41,422
rather than having a long and " safe " career somewhere else .

744
00:58:41,547 --> 00:58:47,630
In Japan , there are no producers like that at all .

745
00:58:50,463 --> 00:58:54,297
So , there are many , many films that come out that are all right ,

746
00:58:54,422 --> 00:58:57,963
but they aren 't very interesting or challenging .

747
00:58:58,088 --> 00:59:03,255
It 's extremely difficult for unique voices to compete in that environment .

748
00:59:03,380 --> 00:59:05,672
Everybody has the same style .

749
00:59:05,797 --> 00:59:09,255
They try to make it appear different by using more distinctive music ,

750
00:59:09,380 --> 00:59:11,797
or fancy cutting or camera movements ,

751
00:59:11,922 --> 00:59:15,672
and they may think that that stuff makes their films unique ,

752
00:59:15,797 --> 00:59:18,255
but I don 't think so , it 's not about that .

753
00:59:20,005 --> 00:59:25,213
So , I was lucky . Miike understood my story .

754
00:59:25,338 --> 00:59:29,588
I wrote the story , but I was always wondering ,

755
00:59:29,713 --> 00:59:34,588
" What are people going to think when they read this ? "

756
00:59:34,713 --> 00:59:36,963
I wondered a little bit .

757
00:59:37,880 --> 00:59:40,797
But I handed it over , turned it in .

758
00:59:40,922 --> 00:59:44,380
I was so excited to meet him and get his reaction .

759
00:59:44,505 --> 00:59:46,797
And he said it was good .

760
00:59:46,922 --> 00:59:52,547
So ... that 's really how it all came together ,

761
00:59:52,672 --> 00:59:54,588
and that made me really happy .

762
00:59:54,713 --> 00:59:57,172
But even with Miike behind it ,

763
00:59:57,297 --> 01:00:01,130
if other people had found it confusing or unclear ,

764
01:00:01,255 --> 01:00:04,297
the movie wouldn 't have received the recognition that it has .

765
01:00:04,422 --> 01:00:07,963
The audience wouldn 't have found it as interesting .

766
01:00:09,088 --> 01:00:14,797
In Japan , it 's pretty difficult to make this kind of film .

767
01:00:17,838 --> 01:00:21,463
What was it like when you made Imprint ?

768
01:00:23,255 --> 01:00:28,088
We knew that might cause problems , but we did it anyway .

769
01:00:28,213 --> 01:00:31,838
It was a shorter-form film called Imprint .

770
01:00:31,963 --> 01:00:37,213
It was close to the original story .

771
01:00:39,047 --> 01:00:42,880
I got the impression that they really wanted me to do something .

772
01:00:43,005 --> 01:00:48,422
They 'd seen Audition overseas .

773
01:00:48,547 --> 01:00:52,672
One good thing about Audition ,

774
01:00:54,130 --> 01:00:59,505
you 'll never know the reason why the movie was made .

775
01:01:00,880 --> 01:01:04,880
That 's the power of it .

776
01:01:05,005 --> 01:01:10,963
For Imprint , we had direction . We knew why we were making it ...

777
01:01:12,213 --> 01:01:16,255
so in that sense , it was easier to make .

778
01:01:16,380 --> 01:01:22,755
That could have been our motivation ... for crossing the line .

779
01:01:22,880 --> 01:01:26,172
I never thought about it like " crossing the line , " though .

780
01:01:26,297 --> 01:01:32,630
Audition was so much scarier .

781
01:01:35,213 --> 01:01:38,838
The story was realistic .

782
01:01:38,963 --> 01:01:43,338
The original story was like that .

783
01:01:43,463 --> 01:01:49,172
Imprint had a story that was kind of set in its own world .

784
01:01:49,297 --> 01:01:54,505
It wasn 't that real-life kind of fear that you feel with Audition .

785
01:02:00,088 --> 01:02:05,630
With Imprint , I think Miike took his enthusiasm to an extreme .

786
01:02:06,297 --> 01:02:08,797
Hey , I just did what the script said to do .

787
01:02:15,380 --> 01:02:18,838
This guy 's the dangerous one .

788
01:02:18,963 --> 01:02:23,338
But it was impossible for American audiences

789
01:02:23,463 --> 01:02:29,463
to understand killing an infant because of poverty ,

790
01:02:29,588 --> 01:02:35,130
or getting an abortion or throwing a baby in the river .

791
01:02:35,255 --> 01:02:40,380
They probably didn 't see any reason for there to be movie about that .

792
01:02:40,505 --> 01:02:45,922
The reaction to Audition between Europe and America was very different .

793
01:02:46,047 --> 01:02:50,547
In London and Paris , there were all these female reporters .

794
01:02:50,672 --> 01:02:52,630
They said that I was a feminist .

795
01:02:52,755 --> 01:02:58,338
They thought , " Hey , she 's getting revenge on these womanizers . "

796
01:02:58,463 --> 01:03:02,713
In New York , I was told by male and female reporters that I was the devil .

797
01:03:02,838 --> 01:03:04,713
I was like , " Really ? "

798
01:03:04,838 --> 01:03:09,797
I wanted to say , " Asami is the devil in this , not me . "

799
01:03:09,922 --> 01:03:15,005
Each country has its own reaction .

800
01:03:15,130 --> 01:03:18,172
They have totally varied perspectives .

801
01:03:18,297 --> 01:03:22,338
Audiences from Japan , Europe and America ,

802
01:03:22,463 --> 01:03:27,797
they all had very particular responses to watching Imprint .

803
01:03:29,380 --> 01:03:33,047
But Tobe Hooper , the director , told me ...

804
01:03:34,547 --> 01:03:38,047
that when there was an announcement in the newspaper

805
01:03:38,172 --> 01:03:41,338
that the film wouldn 't be aired on TV , that 's when I became a real director .

806
01:03:42,505 --> 01:03:45,672
Which was very Tobe Hooper of him to say .

807
01:03:45,797 --> 01:03:48,172
He is awesome .

808
01:03:48,297 --> 01:03:50,713
Yeah , I 'm not sure if that makes me happy or not .

809
01:03:57,713 --> 01:04:04,297
I felt like the plot 's development in this was covered up .

810
01:04:04,422 --> 01:04:10,588
For example , you learn about Asami , but only little by little .

811
01:04:10,713 --> 01:04:14,547
Her strangeness Is revealed very gradually .

812
01:04:14,672 --> 01:04:16,630
It all happens very slowly .

813
01:04:16,755 --> 01:04:22,213
Was that the plan when you were writing ?

814
01:04:22,338 --> 01:04:24,672
Was that what you wanted ,

815
01:04:24,797 --> 01:04:30,088
or did you guys have discussions about the pacing and things like that ?

816
01:04:30,213 --> 01:04:33,422
In the original story ,

817
01:04:33,547 --> 01:04:38,005
it doesn 't show how Asami lives or much of her background ,

818
01:04:38,130 --> 01:04:43,588
but she says things that sound too peculiar to be true ,

819
01:04:43,713 --> 01:04:47,672
and I really wanted to emphasize that aspect in the script .

820
01:04:47,797 --> 01:04:54,005
And I think Miike had a vision of where he wanted to take it .

821
01:04:55,255 --> 01:05:00,380
Yeah , as far as the plot , I left it up to him .

822
01:05:01,213 --> 01:05:06,047
Whenever we 'd meet , I told him to do whatever he wanted to do .

823
01:05:06,172 --> 01:05:10,380
He took the story where he wanted .

824
01:05:10,505 --> 01:05:13,047
You know ...

825
01:05:13,672 --> 01:05:18,838
a screenplay is kind of ...

826
01:05:18,963 --> 01:05:22,672
its own art form ,

827
01:05:22,797 --> 01:05:25,880
separate from the final film .

828
01:05:26,005 --> 01:05:32,880
So it 's up to ... the writer .

829
01:05:33,005 --> 01:05:38,297
In Japan , writers are appreciated for incorporating the opinions and ideas

830
01:05:38,422 --> 01:05:45,088
of the producers and the directors ; not only is that appreciated , it 's expected .

831
01:05:46,005 --> 01:05:48,713
They 're expected to listen to everybody 's opinions .

832
01:05:48,838 --> 01:05:52,005
Of course , when you work that way ...

833
01:05:53,213 --> 01:05:57,880
you have to actually use these ideas from all these other various people .

834
01:05:59,213 --> 01:06:04,047
That 's why I wanted to leave him alone as much as possible .

835
01:06:05,963 --> 01:06:10,005
When I read the story for the first time ...

836
01:06:12,838 --> 01:06:16,463
except the last sequence ...

837
01:06:18,547 --> 01:06:22,922
I thought that it would be relatively easy for me to shoot .

838
01:06:24,505 --> 01:06:26,505
So , for the first hour ,

839
01:06:26,630 --> 01:06:29,755
which is the first 60 pages in the script ,

840
01:06:29,880 --> 01:06:34,672
everything 's pretty normal , there 's no jumping around in time .

841
01:06:34,797 --> 01:06:39,422
As a movie , it 's explanatory , it 's very matter-of-fact ,

842
01:06:39,547 --> 01:06:44,047
and it includes some scenes which could have been cut out

843
01:06:44,172 --> 01:06:49,297
so the audience could get the impression

844
01:06:49,422 --> 01:06:54,963
that this movie is not all it 's cracked up to be .

845
01:06:55,088 --> 01:06:59,547
It was like , if the plot didn 't move forward enough in the next five minutes ,

846
01:06:59,672 --> 01:07:04,380
some audiences would have walked out , and by then it 'd be too late to reach them .

847
01:07:04,505 --> 01:07:06,672
So , it 's almost too late .

848
01:07:06,797 --> 01:07:12,380
They spend the first hour thinking that the film is not what they heard about ,

849
01:07:12,505 --> 01:07:15,255
getting more and more frustrated as it goes along ,

850
01:07:15,380 --> 01:07:22,130
and then , suddenly , the plot develops in a crazy way .

851
01:07:22,255 --> 01:07:26,547
So , I find the plot very uncommon .

852
01:07:27,755 --> 01:07:32,255
But upon reading the story , I could imagine how to make it .

853
01:07:32,380 --> 01:07:37,922
I knew how I should do it right when I read it .

854
01:07:38,047 --> 01:07:41,630
So , I think that 's ...

855
01:07:42,297 --> 01:07:46,213
that 's probably the biggest difference between the original story

856
01:07:46,338 --> 01:07:48,213
and the plot of the screenplay .

857
01:07:48,338 --> 01:07:50,088
What do you think ?

858
01:07:50,213 --> 01:07:55,422
When I write ,

859
01:07:55,547 --> 01:08:00,255
if the story belongs to a certain genre , such as psychological thriller ,

860
01:08:00,380 --> 01:08:04,547
I would have the story develop at a faster pace for the first half .

861
01:08:04,672 --> 01:08:08,672
Maybe put in more action scenes .

862
01:08:08,797 --> 01:08:13,505
I 'd probably use more cinematic plots .

863
01:08:13,630 --> 01:08:19,130
Once I realised this film wouldn 't be like that , I stopped writing .

864
01:08:19,255 --> 01:08:22,338
I realised I might have been going in the wrong direction .

865
01:08:22,463 --> 01:08:25,547
I left the script for two or three days .

866
01:08:25,672 --> 01:08:28,755
I was just waiting for something to hit me .

867
01:08:28,880 --> 01:08:33,922
Then the thought of playing with time occurred to me .

868
01:08:34,047 --> 01:08:37,713
Even if the story goes back and forth in time ,

869
01:08:37,838 --> 01:08:42,380
I thought the audience should still be able to follow it .

870
01:08:43,422 --> 01:08:46,672
And since they were seeing it visually ,

871
01:08:46,797 --> 01:08:50,047
it 'd be less confusing than it would be to read .

872
01:08:50,172 --> 01:08:53,880
Because of CG technology , we can do anything now .

873
01:08:54,005 --> 01:08:57,338
We can control space ,

874
01:08:57,463 --> 01:09:01,130
so the audience isn 't going to be impressed , necessarily ,

875
01:09:01,255 --> 01:09:06,797
regardless of what we do with time and space ; they 've seen it before .

876
01:09:06,922 --> 01:09:12,630
But sitting in a theatre , you 're sharing time with others .

877
01:09:12,755 --> 01:09:16,047
You 're forced to share that time .

878
01:09:16,172 --> 01:09:21,713
So , I thought , if I could work on that , it could be intriguing .

879
01:09:21,838 --> 01:09:24,463
That 's what I was thinking .

880
01:09:25,505 --> 01:09:29,422
As Miike said before ,

881
01:09:29,547 --> 01:09:35,297
we share time when we watch something , and sometimes it 's slow ,

882
01:09:35,422 --> 01:09:40,255
and sometimes it 's fast , and I wanted to control how that felt .

883
01:09:41,297 --> 01:09:44,338
I really wanted the audience to feel an acceleration of time in the last half .

884
01:09:44,463 --> 01:09:47,630
That 's what I wanted to do .

885
01:09:48,505 --> 01:09:54,005
Miike understood that and did his thing .

886
01:09:54,130 --> 01:10:00,380
When I saw this film at a screening ...

887
01:10:01,463 --> 01:10:03,963
I was impressed in a lot of ways .

888
01:10:05,338 --> 01:10:08,130
The story was perfectly visualized .

889
01:10:08,255 --> 01:10:11,005
When I write , it 's the world of imagination .

890
01:10:16,213 --> 01:10:19,088
Here he is . Renji Ishibashi .

891
01:10:19,213 --> 01:10:22,630
He 's kind of a toy in the Japanese film industry .

892
01:10:24,588 --> 01:10:30,963
Everybody has fun playing with him . Just how to play is up to you .

893
01:10:31,088 --> 01:10:37,130
He 'll tell you , " I 'm not your toy , " but he really does enjoy it .

894
01:10:37,255 --> 01:10:41,130
As an actor , he knows what he 's doing .

895
01:10:41,255 --> 01:10:46,130
He 's in several of my movies . I really love him .

896
01:10:48,422 --> 01:10:51,047
Was this film the first thing you did with him ?

897
01:10:51,172 --> 01:10:55,005
We did The Bird People in China .

898
01:10:55,672 --> 01:10:59,380
He 'd say , " I 'm an actor who can handle the shooting ,

899
01:10:59,505 --> 01:11:02,255
" even in remote areas , so don 't worry about it . "

900
01:11:02,380 --> 01:11:08,047
But then he 'd start whining and going , " Do you know I was a child actor ? "

901
01:11:09,338 --> 01:11:12,463
" I can 't handle this anymore . "

902
01:11:12,588 --> 01:11:14,255
He was whining .

903
01:11:15,588 --> 01:11:19,213
I thought that was hilarious .

904
01:11:21,588 --> 01:11:25,422
I really wanted to put him In my movies .

905
01:11:25,547 --> 01:11:27,088
He 's just an enthralling guy .

906
01:11:27,213 --> 01:11:32,380
He 's like a vestige of an older Japanese film industry .

907
01:11:32,505 --> 01:11:36,255
He worked with Shintaro Katsu all the time .

908
01:11:36,380 --> 01:11:40,880
He missed him a lot . He wished Katsu was there .

909
01:11:41,005 --> 01:11:45,963
Although I wasn 't a member

910
01:11:46,088 --> 01:11:52,338
of any of the five major Japanese production companies

911
01:11:52,463 --> 01:11:55,047
and I 've worked freely ,

912
01:11:55,172 --> 01:11:58,588
when I come into contact with someone like him ,

913
01:11:58,713 --> 01:12:04,255
it reminds me that the older generations are not irrelevant to us ,

914
01:12:04,380 --> 01:12:06,005
nor are we to them .

915
01:12:06,963 --> 01:12:10,672
He genuinely loves acting .

916
01:12:13,422 --> 01:12:19,713
He had special makeup on that day .

917
01:12:21,172 --> 01:12:26,630
No , it 's not that day . He had another scene .

918
01:12:28,380 --> 01:12:31,672
He had some crazy makeup on for the scene .

919
01:12:31,797 --> 01:12:34,463
And he had to wait for eight hours .

920
01:12:35,588 --> 01:12:42,047
And there was another guy who was waiting there ,

921
01:12:42,172 --> 01:12:45,422
so he kept on telling the other actor , for eight hours ,

922
01:12:45,547 --> 01:12:50,130
how terrible the shooting of The Bird People in China had been .

923
01:12:50,255 --> 01:12:53,838
So , when I told the actor , " I 'm so sorry we made you wait , " "

924
01:12:53,963 --> 01:12:58,130
he said , " No problem . Thanks to The Bird People in China , I didn 't get bored . "

925
01:12:58,255 --> 01:13:01,088
He was able to talk about it for eight hours .

926
01:13:01,213 --> 01:13:04,130
It must have been tough for the listener , actually .

927
01:13:10,880 --> 01:13:12,630
Had you worked with him , Tengan ?

928
01:13:12,755 --> 01:13:14,463
No , I hadn 't .

929
01:13:18,130 --> 01:13:22,588
This is so confusing .

930
01:13:24,047 --> 01:13:27,713
But we feel something from him . He is awesome .

931
01:13:38,672 --> 01:13:41,755
Let 's talk about the scenes coming up .

932
01:13:41,880 --> 01:13:45,505
It relates to the story .

933
01:13:45,630 --> 01:13:48,838
I think she had experienced some trauma in her childhood ,

934
01:13:48,963 --> 01:13:56,088
and it became this huge part of her . Her love became obsession .

935
01:13:56,213 --> 01:13:58,672
That 's how I look at it .

936
01:13:58,797 --> 01:14:03,880
Her only way of showing love was through torture ,

937
01:14:04,005 --> 01:14:09,088
since she had been tortured when she was a kid

938
01:14:09,213 --> 01:14:11,588
by the Reniji Ishibashi character .

939
01:14:11,713 --> 01:14:15,588
She thought they were the same thing .

940
01:14:15,713 --> 01:14:23,672
That 's why she tortured people she loved as an expression of love .

941
01:14:25,213 --> 01:14:28,630
Yeah , that 's one way to look at it .

942
01:14:28,755 --> 01:14:32,463
She may have some other reasons .

943
01:14:32,588 --> 01:14:35,588
Like ...

944
01:14:39,588 --> 01:14:44,380
I can say that you 're right about her craziness

945
01:14:44,505 --> 01:14:50,547
growing out of the nature of events that happened in her past .

946
01:14:51,630 --> 01:14:54,963
I think those things had an impact on her in very special ways .

947
01:14:55,088 --> 01:14:59,713
To play a role , actors usually ask lots of questions ,

948
01:14:59,838 --> 01:15:01,880
and they try to better understand the character .

949
01:15:02,005 --> 01:15:06,547
But she felt intuitively that she knew how to play this role .

950
01:15:06,672 --> 01:15:11,380
We didn 't ask how she interpreted it , because we liked it that way

951
01:15:11,505 --> 01:15:15,630
and we wanted to hold on to the mystery .

952
01:15:15,755 --> 01:15:19,255
We didn 't force our image of the character on her .

953
01:15:19,380 --> 01:15:24,755
Usually we 're supposed to understand all of the characters in a film .

954
01:15:24,880 --> 01:15:26,547
That 's the director 's job

955
01:15:26,672 --> 01:15:30,380
and the assistant director 's job , I 've been told that all my life .

956
01:15:30,505 --> 01:15:34,297
" You need to understand your characters .

957
01:15:34,422 --> 01:15:36,880
" Use your understanding to direct them . "

958
01:15:37,797 --> 01:15:43,255
But , in this case , I didn 't understand ,

959
01:15:43,380 --> 01:15:46,713
and I couldn 't pretend that I did .

960
01:15:46,838 --> 01:15:51,463
So , when we were not sure about something , we asked Asami .

961
01:15:51,588 --> 01:15:57,338
That 's what we did . It didn 't always clear up our questions .

962
01:15:58,713 --> 01:16:02,630
Even if we got an answer , we couldn 't tell if it was true or not .

963
01:16:02,755 --> 01:16:08,422
Come to think of it , this seems to happen to me a lot .

964
01:16:10,672 --> 01:16:13,880
The idea of cause and effect

965
01:16:14,005 --> 01:16:18,338
Is , of course , easy to understand , and it 's very useful ,

966
01:16:18,463 --> 01:16:24,172
but there are things that are unbelievable .

967
01:16:24,297 --> 01:16:29,630
The past Asami talks about sounds a little different

968
01:16:29,755 --> 01:16:32,338
from what the real story must be .

969
01:16:33,422 --> 01:16:37,338
So ... well ...

970
01:16:38,672 --> 01:16:42,505
Look , Miike visualized something as disgusting as this .

971
01:16:42,630 --> 01:16:49,672
I wrote it , but I wondered if you would actually shoot it .

972
01:16:49,797 --> 01:16:53,213
And he did . He visualizes every single line .

973
01:16:54,088 --> 01:16:57,672
When I saw this scene , I realised that he shot everything .

974
01:16:59,630 --> 01:17:00,963
So , it was written in the script .

975
01:17:01,088 --> 01:17:03,047
Yes , it was .

976
01:17:03,172 --> 01:17:06,088
But a lot of directors don 't or won 't shoot everything ,

977
01:17:06,213 --> 01:17:09,505
and some will get mad if I don 't write out every little detail .

978
01:17:09,630 --> 01:17:14,047
But this guy shoots everything that 's in the script .

979
01:17:14,172 --> 01:17:16,338
Did that make you happy ?

980
01:17:16,463 --> 01:17:18,963
Well ... I 'm not sure .

981
01:17:20,963 --> 01:17:23,713
I felt , sometimes , that I could have written some parts better .

982
01:17:25,005 --> 01:17:27,672
But I didn 't feel like there was anything unnecessary .

983
01:17:33,630 --> 01:17:38,130
The director of photography was Hideo Yamamoto , right ?

984
01:17:38,255 --> 01:17:39,338
Yes .

985
01:17:39,463 --> 01:17:41,922
Had you worked with him before ?

986
01:17:42,047 --> 01:17:45,047
When I was making " V-Cinema "

987
01:17:45,713 --> 01:17:51,297
and I was an assistant director ,

988
01:17:51,422 --> 01:17:54,713
he did a lot of work on set , mostly as a camera assistant .

989
01:17:54,838 --> 01:17:58,422
Unlike in America , we have to climb up the career ladder .

990
01:17:58,547 --> 01:18:04,880
The person in the lowest position loads film ,

991
01:18:05,005 --> 01:18:08,880
then starts pulling focus after learning some stuff , then adjusts apertures ,

992
01:18:09,005 --> 01:18:13,922
then becomes chief , then finally becomes a camera operator .

993
01:18:15,547 --> 01:18:19,213
That 's how the Japanese film industry works .

994
01:18:19,338 --> 01:18:22,755
And I was the third assistant director , and doing slating .

995
01:18:22,880 --> 01:18:27,338
Then I was the second , then chief , then finally I became a director .

996
01:18:27,463 --> 01:18:31,005
In the camera department , the climb to the top is a bit faster ,

997
01:18:31,130 --> 01:18:35,047
but it 's close , it 's almost the same .

998
01:18:40,005 --> 01:18:42,088
I think we went to the same school , too .

999
01:18:42,213 --> 01:18:44,630
- Right . - Yokohama .

1000
01:18:44,755 --> 01:18:49,505
I think we were in the same year or something .

1001
01:18:51,755 --> 01:18:53,463
I knew his name .

1002
01:18:54,338 --> 01:18:56,172
But you didn 't meet until you were both on the set .

1003
01:18:56,297 --> 01:18:59,713
Right . We met when we were still assistants .

1004
01:18:59,838 --> 01:19:06,255
One day , we were going , " Wow , we came all that way . "

1005
01:19:06,380 --> 01:19:09,297
But it 's not like ...

1006
01:19:10,338 --> 01:19:13,130
we were best friends or anything .

1007
01:19:13,255 --> 01:19:19,380
We eat together sometimes , but we don 't know each other real well .

1008
01:19:19,505 --> 01:19:23,338
We 're not aware of each other 's personal lives .

1009
01:19:23,463 --> 01:19:26,755
When I want to respect someone ...

1010
01:19:27,880 --> 01:19:32,005
I want to keep a good image of them in my head ,

1011
01:19:32,130 --> 01:19:39,338
and I think knowing them too well can be detrimental to the work .

1012
01:19:39,463 --> 01:19:42,213
The more we just work together ,

1013
01:19:42,338 --> 01:19:48,630
the further my image probably goes away from the reality , I imagine .

1014
01:19:50,047 --> 01:19:55,130
But he 's very flexible .

1015
01:19:55,255 --> 01:19:59,255
He has some hang-up about death .

1016
01:19:59,380 --> 01:20:06,547
Yamamoto shot one of Takeshi Kitano 's films .

1017
01:20:06,672 --> 01:20:08,755
It was Fireworks .

1018
01:20:08,880 --> 01:20:14,338
His films are very different .

1019
01:20:14,463 --> 01:20:20,172
He almost died once . It might 've been an attempted suicide .

1020
01:20:20,297 --> 01:20:23,297
But he recovered and started shooting films .

1021
01:20:23,422 --> 01:20:26,838
So , he had a near-death experience .

1022
01:20:26,963 --> 01:20:35,297
What 's interesting about Hideo Yamamoto is ... he prays .

1023
01:20:36,547 --> 01:20:41,588
His father and mother both died early on in his life .

1024
01:20:41,713 --> 01:20:44,672
She died right after he was born .

1025
01:20:44,797 --> 01:20:48,547
None of his relatives are living ,

1026
01:20:48,672 --> 01:20:54,172
so I think he thought that when he got to be his parents ' age , he 'd die .

1027
01:20:54,297 --> 01:20:58,838
He could 've been thinking that since he was a kid .

1028
01:20:58,963 --> 01:21:05,422
It 's natural . He 's tried to forget it , but can 't .

1029
01:21:05,547 --> 01:21:07,588
That 's who he is .

1030
01:21:07,713 --> 01:21:11,463
So , whatever life he 's led past that age has been like extra time .

1031
01:21:11,588 --> 01:21:16,047
He 's okay , too , he 's very healthy .

1032
01:21:16,172 --> 01:21:20,963
But that 's not something anyone can just learn .

1033
01:21:21,088 --> 01:21:25,755
You can 't create that in the frame

1034
01:21:25,880 --> 01:21:28,963
or in the lighting .

1035
01:21:29,088 --> 01:21:33,422
But how he lives definitely shows in the picture ,

1036
01:21:33,547 --> 01:21:41,380
no matter what he shoots , it shows right there .

1037
01:21:41,505 --> 01:21:44,505
For me , it had to be Hideo Yamamoto .

1038
01:21:44,630 --> 01:21:50,630
He doesn 't want to do anything eccentric .

1039
01:21:50,755 --> 01:21:56,880
He doesn 't try to make a good picture .

1040
01:21:57,005 --> 01:22:00,463
He regrets it when he makes a good picture .

1041
01:22:00,588 --> 01:22:04,588
He likes simple shooting .

1042
01:22:04,713 --> 01:22:10,755
I don 't think he likes being told , " That was a great picture . "

1043
01:22:10,880 --> 01:22:17,338
I think he likes making a good film , not creating a good picture .

1044
01:22:17,463 --> 01:22:20,922
I really appreciate that approach .

1045
01:22:22,963 --> 01:22:27,088
So , do you work with him often ?

1046
01:22:27,213 --> 01:22:29,963
Yes , we do .

1047
01:22:30,088 --> 01:22:34,922
We did Yatterman together .

1048
01:22:35,047 --> 01:22:39,213
That had nothing to do with death , though .

1049
01:22:49,505 --> 01:22:55,672
I heard you had a promotional tour for Audition .

1050
01:22:55,797 --> 01:22:58,422
Was that in 2001 ?

1051
01:22:58,547 --> 01:22:59,880
Right .

1052
01:23:00,005 --> 01:23:02,088
And then 9 / 11 happened .

1053
01:23:02,213 --> 01:23:07,838
Right , it was the same time .

1054
01:23:07,963 --> 01:23:13,672
We were planning to attend a film festival in Toronto .

1055
01:23:13,797 --> 01:23:18,005
We were going to do a promotion for / chi the Killer .

1056
01:23:18,130 --> 01:23:23,505
The promotion came with barf bags .

1057
01:23:23,630 --> 01:23:30,755
It was this big event where the audience received barf bags .

1058
01:23:30,880 --> 01:23:33,505
Anyway , we were going to go over there for this promotion .

1059
01:23:33,630 --> 01:23:39,838
Right before that , it was going to be released in Los Angeles .

1060
01:23:39,963 --> 01:23:42,963
So , we stopped off in Los Angeles for this other promotion .

1061
01:23:43,088 --> 01:23:46,047
We went to Los Angeles and promoted our film ,

1062
01:23:46,172 --> 01:23:51,255
and on the day we were going to fly to Toronto ,

1063
01:23:51,380 --> 01:23:57,838
we found out something terrible had happened in New York .

1064
01:24:01,047 --> 01:24:06,380
Of course , our flight was cancelled , and we couldn 't go to Toronto .

1065
01:24:06,505 --> 01:24:10,463
They were wondering if they should even continue the festival .

1066
01:24:10,588 --> 01:24:17,755
They decided to continue with it , to show that the terrorists had not won .

1067
01:24:17,880 --> 01:24:20,338
I thought that was great .

1068
01:24:21,172 --> 01:24:28,047
We were going to go back to Japan on a flight out of San Francisco .

1069
01:24:28,172 --> 01:24:34,255
So , as we 're driving from Los Angeles to San Francisco ,

1070
01:24:34,380 --> 01:24:41,380
we found out Dead or Alive was going to be released that same day .

1071
01:24:42,255 --> 01:24:48,755
They invited us to come and promote that ,

1072
01:24:49,255 --> 01:24:54,172
so we showed up and did an audience meet-and-greet .

1073
01:24:55,672 --> 01:25:00,463
That was a strange series of experiences .

1074
01:25:05,005 --> 01:25:07,672
It 's totally off-topic ,

1075
01:25:08,880 --> 01:25:14,797
but Imamura 's last work was September 11 ,

1076
01:25:14,922 --> 01:25:18,505
and Tengan wrote the screenplay .

1077
01:25:18,630 --> 01:25:24,630
I guess you guys made the film together , right ?

1078
01:25:24,755 --> 01:25:30,505
When I heard about the project ...

1079
01:25:32,963 --> 01:25:38,755
I thought it would be great . I thought it was a great idea .

1080
01:25:38,880 --> 01:25:42,880
It was something inevitable .

1081
01:25:43,005 --> 01:25:47,463
Imamura wasn 't expecting anything like that .

1082
01:25:47,588 --> 01:25:51,422
Right . The project was brought to him .

1083
01:25:53,255 --> 01:26:00,130
But he really felt something for it , and decided to accept it .

1084
01:26:01,838 --> 01:26:08,338
This was great . Tomorowo Taguchi was incredible .

1085
01:26:08,463 --> 01:26:14,130
It was an almost inhuman performance .

1086
01:26:14,255 --> 01:26:16,130
I think that film is a masterpiece .

1087
01:26:18,422 --> 01:26:21,130
And you wrote the screenplay , Tengan ?

1088
01:26:21,255 --> 01:26:24,922
Yes . One of the stories .

1089
01:26:28,755 --> 01:26:33,255
And they brought the project to you ?

1090
01:26:33,380 --> 01:26:35,005
Yeah , yeah .

1091
01:26:35,130 --> 01:26:39,963
It was 11 directors from around the world all working together on one movie .

1092
01:26:40,088 --> 01:26:47,422
And the theme was the attacks ?

1093
01:26:47,547 --> 01:26:49,297
No , it wasn 't .

1094
01:26:51,380 --> 01:26:56,672
There was this piece of Chinese poetry .

1095
01:26:57,672 --> 01:27:03,213
A author named Masuji Ibuse had translated it ,

1096
01:27:03,338 --> 01:27:06,797
and Imamura added one line to the end .

1097
01:27:06,922 --> 01:27:11,130
He wanted to make a film out of this poem .

1098
01:27:11,255 --> 01:27:15,880
He wanted to do it with Ken Ogata ,

1099
01:27:16,005 --> 01:27:19,255
and he left the story part to me .

1100
01:27:19,380 --> 01:27:24,797
He was like , " I want to shoot this . The story is up to you . "

1101
01:27:31,755 --> 01:27:37,380
You can 't tell who this guy is . Even Japanese people can 't tell .

1102
01:27:37,505 --> 01:27:42,713
We talked about Fireworks , which was shot by Yamamoto ,

1103
01:27:43,380 --> 01:27:46,463
and this guy was the main character in that .

1104
01:27:48,713 --> 01:27:53,005
The movie received the Golden Lion at the Venice Film Festival .

1105
01:27:53,130 --> 01:27:55,338
His name is Ren Osugi .

1106
01:27:56,130 --> 01:28:04,422
He 's received all kinds of awards for best actor in Japan .

1107
01:28:06,130 --> 01:28:09,713
When he eats vomit , he says , " Yummy . "

1108
01:28:09,838 --> 01:28:14,963
He is also a " toy " type , like Ishibashi .

1109
01:28:18,297 --> 01:28:21,130
Could you believe it when he accepted this role ?

1110
01:28:21,255 --> 01:28:28,630
Well ... many actors are actually ...

1111
01:28:28,755 --> 01:28:35,172
not very happy about the roles they usually get .

1112
01:28:35,297 --> 01:28:41,213
The more popular they become , the more likable roles they have to play .

1113
01:28:41,338 --> 01:28:46,297
They get typecast as these likable guys , even when they 're bad guys .

1114
01:28:47,005 --> 01:28:50,130
And when it 's a good guy everybody has to feel sympathy for him .

1115
01:28:50,255 --> 01:28:54,963
Most actors are outlaws .

1116
01:28:55,088 --> 01:29:01,797
Many of them feel like , " I didn 't become an actor to say this stupid line . "

1117
01:29:03,380 --> 01:29:07,588
I think he really wanted to play this kind of role .

1118
01:29:07,713 --> 01:29:13,797
This is so terrible . He 's only wearing briefs .

1119
01:29:23,797 --> 01:29:26,505
This was the special makeup scene , right ?

1120
01:29:26,630 --> 01:29:28,963
We talked about the eight hours , right ?

1121
01:29:29,088 --> 01:29:31,422
This was the scene they waited for .

1122
01:29:31,547 --> 01:29:37,088
The guy in the bag , Ren Osugi , he was the listener .

1123
01:29:37,713 --> 01:29:42,338
That 's a scarier situation than the story in the movie .

1124
01:29:42,463 --> 01:29:44,422
How frightening .

1125
01:29:45,922 --> 01:29:51,338
The frustration exploded in the form of great acting ,

1126
01:29:51,463 --> 01:29:54,255
and that made the movie scarier .

1127
01:29:59,338 --> 01:30:03,547
Somewhere around here , as Tengan talked about before ,

1128
01:30:03,672 --> 01:30:07,172
the story starts going back and forth in time .

1129
01:30:09,630 --> 01:30:17,338
There was order up until this point , but now it 's starting to get messed up .

1130
01:30:17,463 --> 01:30:21,422
The characters start losing control .

1131
01:30:21,547 --> 01:30:26,797
Directing these scenes , it 's like something was driving me .

1132
01:30:26,922 --> 01:30:29,922
We couldn 't discuss it too much .

1133
01:30:30,047 --> 01:30:35,172
The story made sense to us somehow ,

1134
01:30:35,297 --> 01:30:40,963
and we didn 't want to change that feeling .

1135
01:30:48,172 --> 01:30:55,963
Did you shoot the scenes in the order they were scripted ?

1136
01:30:56,088 --> 01:31:01,922
In order ? There 's no way to shoot in order .

1137
01:31:02,047 --> 01:31:08,380
We have to shoot everything for each set one set at a time .

1138
01:31:08,505 --> 01:31:14,547
It gets all out of order , but that 's inevitable .

1139
01:31:15,172 --> 01:31:20,255
It 's inconvenient , but there are also good things about it .

1140
01:31:21,505 --> 01:31:23,505
We have control over the movie .

1141
01:31:23,630 --> 01:31:26,963
It gives us a measure of control over the actors .

1142
01:31:27,088 --> 01:31:32,130
If they don 't follow instructions , there will be continuity problems .

1143
01:31:33,463 --> 01:31:41,963
So , I can keep my film under control . For the most part .

1144
01:31:46,505 --> 01:31:49,255
Oh , the scene where he gets killed .

1145
01:31:50,297 --> 01:31:52,797
She looks very happy for some reason .

1146
01:32:02,588 --> 01:32:07,047
Cutting meat . Just cutting meat here .

1147
01:32:08,547 --> 01:32:13,505
For the last scene ...

1148
01:32:13,630 --> 01:32:17,547
the sound guy , Kenji Shibazaki , did a really good job .

1149
01:32:17,672 --> 01:32:20,172
He 's done most of my films ,

1150
01:32:20,297 --> 01:32:25,547
and he 's probably done half of all Japanese films , really .

1151
01:32:25,672 --> 01:32:28,338
Yeah , most of my films , too .

1152
01:32:29,338 --> 01:32:33,047
He 's a really strange guy .

1153
01:32:35,213 --> 01:32:39,880
Like a little boy sometimes .

1154
01:32:41,547 --> 01:32:47,588
He makes weird sounds and noises that other people can 't make .

1155
01:32:48,380 --> 01:32:52,713
He was in the studio for a long time for this scene .

1156
01:32:52,838 --> 01:32:57,380
The scene was like this , she 's cutting through flesh ,

1157
01:32:57,505 --> 01:33:00,297
